records:
  - id: "ADR-0001"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "stack.effects-runtime"
        scope: "effect-stack"
        choice: "Effect TS"
        first: true
        reason: "One effect type for errors, dependency injection and concurrency"
  - id: "ADR-0002"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "stack.sql-layer"
        scope: "effect-stack"
        choice: "@effect/sql"
        first: true
        reason: "A query builder outside the effect type re-introduces the boundary Effect removes"
  - id: "ADR-0003"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "stack.http-server"
        scope: "effect-stack"
        choice: "@effect/platform HttpServer"
        first: true
        reason: "Verified preference 2026-05-19; alternatives weighed and named"
  - id: "ADR-0004"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "stack.http-client"
        scope: "effect-stack"
        choice: "@effect/platform HttpClient"
        first: true
        reason: "A bare fetch loses retries, timeouts, tracing and the error channel"
  - id: "ADR-0005"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "stack.web-framework"
        scope: "effect-stack"
        choice: "React Router, framework mode, SSR"
        first: true
        reason: "One rendering model across four apps rather than four"
  - id: "ADR-0006"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "ui.design-system"
        scope: "duro-stack"
        choice: "@duro-app/ui"
        first: true
        reason: "Six repositories carried an identical prose block with no owner"
  - id: "ADR-0007"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "ui.policy-enforcement"
        scope: "duro-stack"
        choice: "@duro-app/eslint-config"
        first: true
        reason: "The older plugin-plus-preset spelling survives in two repositories"
  - id: "ADR-0008"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "release.trigger"
        choice: "A pushed version tag, never a merge"
        first: true
        reason: "Merging must be safe; publishing must be deliberate"
  - id: "ADR-0009"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "ci.test-gate-stage"
        choice: "pre-commit"
        first: true
        reason: "git holds the remote connection open across the whole pre-push hook"
  - id: "ADR-0010"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "code.paradigm"
        choice: "Functions and composition; no inheritance hierarchies"
        first: true
        reason: "A composed function is testable alone; a subclass is testable only with its parents"
  - id: "ADR-0011"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "code.practice-canon"
        choice: "Robert C. Martin's Clean Code and Clean Architecture, read for Rust, Go and TypeScript"
        first: true
        reason: 'A named canon can be cited by rule in review; "write it well" cannot'
  - id: "ADR-0012"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "release.version-scheme"
        choice: "Semantic Versioning 2.0.0"
        first: true
        reason: "A consumer must be able to tell from the number alone whether a bump can break it"
  - id: "ADR-0013"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "data.layering"
        choice: "Medallion — bronze as received, silver conformed, gold consumer-shaped"
        first: true
        reason: "Raw data kept immutable is the only thing that makes every later layer reproducible"
  - id: "ADR-0014"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "ui.interaction-model"
        choice: "Micro-interactions on every action; game-like feedback, progress and reward"
        first: true
        reason: "An interface that answers every action is one the person trusts and keeps using"
  - id: "ADR-0015"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "code.functional-canon"
        choice: "Grokking Simplicity and Domain Modeling Made Functional, with Functional Programming in Scala and Functional Design as depth"
        first: true
        reason: "ADR-0010 chose the paradigm; a paradigm without a named canon is re-derived at every review"
  - id: "ADR-0016"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "ui.mockup-handoff"
        scope: "duro-stack"
        choice: "Token-annotated artboards; component and token names bind, pixel values do not"
        first: true
        reason: "Mockups were drawn outside the token system and each session re-decided how faithful an implementation had to be"
  - id: "ADR-0017"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "ci.presence"
        choice: "A continuous-integration workflow from the first commit, running the same check the workstation runs"
        first: true
        reason: "A repository whose only gate is a hook on one machine has no gate at all on the day that machine is not the one pushing"
      - key: "ci.system"
        choice: "GitHub Actions for an open-source repository, Forgejo Actions for a private one"
        first: true
        reason: "The CI system follows where the repository is published; visibility decides that, so it decides this"
  - id: "ADR-0018"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "guidance.generated-block"
        choice: "Regenerated by the commit gate of the tool that owns it and carried by the change that triggered it, never by a commit to main"
        first: true
        reason: "A generated block an agent reads and believes is only true when the gate that generates it also stages it"
  - id: "ADR-0019"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "build.toolchain-source"
        choice: "The repository pins its language toolchain and its build tools, exactly, in the file its own check targets read — never the machine's PATH"
        first: true
        reason: "A check that ran on a different compiler or linter than CI's is a claim about a different program; only an exact per-repository pin makes the workstation and CI run the same binaries"
  - id: "ADR-0020"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "change.dependency-bar"
        choice: "Ownership, and none on a trust path — a dependency is added when owning its code would cost more than owning the dependency, argued beside its declaration; code that runs unattended with someone's credentials defaults to no dependencies, checked on the resolved tree"
        first: true
        reason: "Without a bar, the cheapest-looking rung of a ladder is always the one below it, and the cost arrives later as somebody else's abandoned package"
      - key: "change.deferral-record"
        choice: "A `holds-until:` comment in the code, naming the limit it holds to and the condition that ends it"
        first: true
        reason: "A deferral recorded somewhere other than the code it defers is a deferral nobody meets again until it breaks"
  - id: "ADR-0021"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "cli.conventions"
        choice: "The Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev) are the canon, with the POSIX Utility Syntax Guidelines and GNU long options as the syntax floor"
        first: true
        reason: "Five first-party command lines each re-decided help, exit codes, colour and prompts on their own; a named canon can be cited by section in review, and POSIX is what every shell and script already assumes"
  - id: "ADR-0022"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "work.plan-record"
        choice: "An approved plan is the task branch's first commit, under docs/plans/, living while active and frozen once done, abandoned or imported"
        first: true
        reason: "A plan kept outside the repository is lost at the end of the session that approved it, and every later session re-derives it from memory"
  - id: "ADR-0023"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "work.preview-approval"
        choice: "A UI-changing commit is shown to the person on localhost after the agent's own verification, and published only after the person approves that exact commit"
        first: true
        reason: "An agent checking its own screen is not the person looking at it, and a UI defect found after deploy costs a fix pull request and a release"
  - id: "ADR-0024"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "ci.attested-skip"
        choice: "A CI step is skipped only on a verified attestation that the same declared command, run by the same tool, passed on the exact tree CI checked out; steps that produce what ships are never skipped"
        first: true
        reason: "Laptop-attested test skips saved about 40 hours of CI step time in six weeks, and lint can be proven the same way — but only a proof of the same command on the same tree is evidence, anything looser is a skip nobody checked"
  - id: "ADR-0025"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "ui.text-source"
        choice: "The repository's i18n catalog, with its English entry, looked up through the catalog's lookup function, as the reference value wherever a string has to be named; no literal in any language where the catalog has the string, and never the key in its place"
        first: true
        reason: "Tests and components in localized repositories each re-decided between hardcoded English, hardcoded English-or-French alternations and raw keys, and each choice drifted from the catalog the person actually reads"
  - id: "ADR-0026"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "guidance.session-load"
        choice: "An index — one line per item, with its id; detail is fetched on demand"
        first: true
        reason: "The decisions repository's session hook put 31,450 bytes, about 8,000 tokens, into every session, mostly rule detail"
      - key: "guidance.prose-canon"
        choice: "The writing rules of ASD-STE100 (Simplified Technical English), with a meaning floor that compression never crosses"
        first: true
        reason: 'A named rule set with word limits can be checked in review; "be concise" cannot, and agent prose grew without a limit'
  - id: "ADR-0027"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "ui.mockup-handoff"
        scope: "duro-stack"
        choice: "Token-annotated artboards; component names and every design value, sizes and border widths included, bind through tokens; layout positions do not"
        replaces:
          - "ADR-0016"
        reason: "Mockups were drawn outside the token system and each session re-decided how faithful an implementation had to be; a touch-target literal in Duro 4.5.0 passed 9 plan reviewers and 4 implementation reviews, because no rule named sizes"
      - key: "ui.measures"
        scope: "duro-stack"
        choice: "Every measure in design-system and consumer styles is a published Duro token: spacing, radius, size and border width; the exemptions are named"
        first: true
        reason: "Duro had no size or border tokens, so every fixed size in 25 components was a literal that no lint rule and no review rule could cite"
  - id: "ADR-0028"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "work.preview-approval"
        choice: "A UI-changing commit is published only after the person approves that exact commit on localhost, unless the plan the person approved declares in its body that the evidence leaves nothing to judge; the guide and the evidence then go in the pull request"
        replaces:
          - "ADR-0023"
        reason: "The person was asked to approve a design-system release whose only visible change they had already decided, and then asked again because the question missed a label; a question with nothing left to judge spends their attention and teaches them to stop reading"
  - id: "ADR-0029"
    status: "accepted"
    decisions:
      - key: "work.landing"
        choice: "The agent carries an approved plan to production — it merges each pull request once CI is green and the implementation review approves, cuts the release a later phase needs, and runs the post-deploy phases; the person is asked only what the plan left open"
        first: true
        reason: "In 22 release questions over a month the person approved the release every time and never chose the hold option on offer; each question cost an interruption and changed no outcome, and an agent waiting for a merge it could make leaves approved work unshipped"
rules:
  - id: "architecture.boundary-data-is-owned"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "Data crossing a boundary is a simple structure the inner side defines, and control crossing outward goes through an interface declared in the inner circle and implemented in the outer one."
    body: |
      **⇢** The use-case crate owns the `Repository` trait; the persistence crate
      implements it and depends on the use-case crate. A database row, a
      framework request type or a generated client type never crosses inward —
      see `data.rows-stay-in-the-adapter` in the Clean Code rules for where the
      mapping lives.
  - id: "architecture.business-rules"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "An entity embodies critical business rules and data that would exist even without the system; a use case describes application-specific rules — input, output, steps, entities involved — and neither knows how it is presented or stored."
  - id: "architecture.dependency-rule"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "Source-code dependencies point inward, toward higher-level policy: nothing in an inner circle knows anything about an outer circle — not a name, not a function, not a data format."
    body: |
      Software is **policy** (the business rules) and **detail** (everything that
      lets the policy run: the database, the web, frameworks, devices).
      Architecture keeps the details irrelevant to the policy for as long as
      possible. The circles, from inside out: **Entities** (enterprise-wide
      business rules and their data), **Use Cases** (application-specific rules
      orchestrating entities), **Interface Adapters** (controllers, presenters,
      gateways: converting between the use-case format and the external format),
      **Frameworks and Drivers** (the web, the database, the UI, devices). The
      count of circles is not the point; the direction is. **⇢** At the module
      boundary: a use-case crate never imports a persistence crate; in Effect the
      service tag is declared beside the use case and the layer beside the
      driver.
  - id: "architecture.draw-boundaries-deliberately"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "Boundaries are drawn where the axis of change lies, between things that matter and things that do not; drawing one costs, drawing none costs more later, and a partial boundary is a legitimate, recorded choice."
    body: |
      A one-dimensional boundary, a facade, a skipped last step: each is fine when
      the full cost is not yet justified, and the choice is written down. Not four
      mandatory layers, not a `usecases` directory in every service, not an
      interface for everything: the rule is the direction of dependencies and the
      isolation of policy from detail, with ceremony proportionate to the size of
      the system.
  - id: "architecture.humble-object"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "Every hard-to-test behaviour is split into a humble object too simple to test — the view, the database gateway, the device driver — and a testable one that holds all the logic — the presenter, the interactor."
    body: |
      The view model carries strings and flags the view merely displays.
  - id: "architecture.independence"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "A good architecture makes the use cases visible, supports the operational needs, and leaves development, deployment and the choice of UI, database and framework independently changeable."
    body: |
      Layers are decoupled along the axes that change for different reasons: use
      case, operation, development, deployment. Decoupling modes: source-level
      (modules in one deployable), deployment-level (separately deployable units),
      service-level (processes over a network). Start at source level; move
      outward only when the operational need arrives, and let the mode change
      without rewriting.
  - id: "architecture.main-is-a-plugin"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "The main component is the ultimate detail: the dirtiest, lowest-level component, where everything is constructed and injected, and a plugin to the application that may exist once per configuration."
    body: |
      **⇢** `main.rs`, `main.go`, the Effect program entry that assembles the
      layers. The constraint form of this rule is `systems.construction-in-main`
      in the Clean Code rules.
  - id: "architecture.screaming"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "The top level of the repository says what the system does, not what it is built with."
    body: |
      Orders, positions, sites — not controllers, models, handlers. A reader
      should be able to tell a health system from an accounting system from the
      directory names.
  - id: "architecture.services-are-not-an-architecture"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "Splitting a system into services neither decouples them nor guarantees independent development; the architectural boundaries still have to be drawn inside and across them."
    body: |
      Services share data formats and cross-cutting features. The book is
      explicit that services are a deployment option, not an architecture; the
      same holds for microservices.
  - id: "architecture.test-boundary"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "Tests are the outermost component of the system: they depend inward, nothing depends on them, and they are designed for through a testing API that drives the use cases directly rather than through volatile details."
    body: |
      Tests coupled to the GUI or to private structure are fragile.
  - id: "boundaries.learning-tests"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Unfamiliar libraries are learned through learning tests, which are kept so that an upgrade reveals a behavioural change; boundary conditions and integration assumptions are tested explicitly."
  - id: "boundaries.own-the-interface"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "A third-party API is wrapped behind an interface this codebase owns, in one place, so that it does not spread through the application and can be replaced."
    body: |
      **⇢** A Go interface declared by the consuming package, a Rust trait in the
      policy crate, an Effect service tag. Boundary-specific translation lives in
      one place; the amount of code that knows external details is minimised;
      application concepts stay independent of vendor-specific models. A
      dependency that does not yet exist is programmed against an interface that
      says what the application needs.
  - id: "boundaries.validate-untrusted-input"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "Every value that enters from outside — a request, a file, a queue message, an environment variable, a database row — is validated into a domain type at the boundary, and nothing downstream re-validates."
    body: |
      **Effect:** `Schema.decodeUnknown` at the edge, once, into the branded and
      structured types the domain uses. **Rust:** `serde` into a type whose
      constructor validates, or `TryFrom` at the edge. **Go:** a decode step
      into a struct followed by one validating constructor per domain type.
      *Domain Modeling Made Functional*'s "parse, don't validate": after the
      boundary, the types carry the proof.
  - id: "change.a-deferral-names-its-ceiling"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0020"
    source: "The fleet's own manifests (attest, amont-runtime, aval-core, relais), read against the ponytail ruleset and its agentic benchmark (github.com/dietrichgebert/ponytail, 2026-06)"
    statement: "A simplification chosen in the knowledge that it will not hold — a global lock, a quadratic scan over a list expected to grow, a heuristic standing in for a rule — carries a `holds-until:` comment naming the limit it holds to and the condition that ends it:"
    body: |
      ```ts
      // holds-until: ~10 writes/second — one writer today; per-key locks after
      ```

      - The marker is one token that no language in the fleet would accept as an
        identifier, so the ledger is a grep: `grep -rn 'holds-until:'` is the
        whole of the tooling, and a deferral that is not greppable is not
        recorded. `ceiling:` was rejected for the job because it already names
        struct fields in amont and relais.
      - It names a choice, never an unknown. Code that is simple because simple is
        correct carries no marker, and a marker names no work for somebody else to
        find — it names the condition under which this stops being right.
      - The fleet carries no `TODO` markers today, and aval denies `todo!()` at
        lint level; the one deferral on record is prose — amont's secrets check
        names a two-megabyte limit and its reason, not what ends it. This rule
        does not ask for more deferrals; it asks that the ones already made in
        prose carry their trigger.
      - A marker is not a licence against the rule above it. A deferral of
        validation at a trust boundary, of error handling that prevents data loss,
        or of a security control is refused rather than marked.

      This is the `TODO` that `comments.code-first` calls actionable — actionable
      because it carries its own trigger — and it is kept accurate or deleted with
      the code it describes, like every other comment
      (`comments.accurate-or-deleted`).
  - id: "change.a-new-dependency-is-owned"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0020"
    source: "The fleet's own manifests (attest, amont-runtime, aval-core, relais), read against the ponytail ruleset and its agentic benchmark (github.com/dietrichgebert/ponytail, 2026-06)"
    statement: "A third-party dependency is added with its argument written beside it: why the rungs above it did not hold, and what the fleet now owns — the transitive tree it pulls in, whether it has a build script, its release cadence, and what happens to this repository the day it is abandoned. The argument sits next to the declaration where the manifest takes a comment (`Cargo.toml`, `go.mod`, `pyproject.toml`), and in the pull request only where it cannot (`package.json`). A dependency is added when owning its code would cost more than owning the dependency — that comparison, not the line count of the call site, is the bar."
    body: |
      - Under the bar: a package behind a one-line call, a package whose one used
        function is in the standard library, a package added to avoid writing a
        loop.
      - Over it: anything on the list the rule above exempts, a format with a
        specification, a protocol client, a substantial and maintained body of
        code the fleet would otherwise write and then have to keep correct.
      - The bar rises with where the code runs. Code that runs unattended with
        somebody's credentials — a commit hook, a CI verifier, anything that
        decides whether a check may be skipped — defaults to no dependencies at
        all, and the default is checked on the resolved tree, not the manifest,
        because a dependency reaches that path through a workspace crate as easily
        as directly. It is a default to argue against in writing, not a wall.
      - Removal is the same decision run backwards, and needs no ceremony: a
        dependency whose last caller went away leaves with it, in that change.
      - A dependency that clears the bar is then wrapped and learned like any
        other boundary: `boundaries.own-the-interface` and
        `boundaries.learning-tests` (ADR-0011) apply from the first call.

      **⇢** amont's `scripts/check-no-deps.sh` fails closed on `cargo tree` of
      the hook binary; relais's crate manifest carries one comment per dependency
      saying what it buys and what it pulls; `uqr` entered duro-app described in
      the pull request as "inline SVG, zero deps", because `package.json` cannot
      say it.
  - id: "change.fix-at-the-defects-locus"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0020"
    source: "The fleet's own manifests (attest, amont-runtime, aval-core, relais), read against the ponytail ruleset and its agentic benchmark (github.com/dietrichgebert/ponytail, 2026-06)"
    statement: "A report names where a defect was *noticed*. The fix goes where the defect *lives* — the shared function every caller reaches, changed once — and every caller of a function being changed is read before it is changed."
    body: |
      - A guard added at one call site is a claim that the other call sites are
        correct. Making that claim without reading them is how a sibling caller
        stays broken while the report is closed.
      - One guard in the shared function is a smaller diff than one per caller, so
        this is rarely a trade against the rules above; where it is, correctness
        wins.
      - Where the shared function genuinely cannot change — it is a dependency's,
        or its callers legitimately want different behaviour — the caller-local
        fix is the right one, and the pull request says which of the two cases
        this is. What is refused is not the local fix; it is the local fix chosen
        by not looking.
  - id: "change.reach-before-adding"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0020"
    source: "The fleet's own manifests (attest, amont-runtime, aval-core, relais), read against the ponytail ruleset and its agentic benchmark (github.com/dietrichgebert/ponytail, 2026-06)"
    statement: "Before code is written to solve a problem, the nearest thing that already solves it is used, in this order: a helper already in this repository, the standard library, a feature the runtime already has, a tool the code's environment already guarantees run as a subprocess, a dependency already in the manifest. Writing it is the rung below those, and a new dependency is the rung below that."
    body: |
      - The order runs after the problem is understood, not instead of
        understanding it. The smallest change in the wrong place is a second
        defect, not a saving.
      - Two approaches of the same size are not equal: the one that is correct at
        the edges wins. Fewer lines breaks a tie; it is never the criterion.
      - A host tool is a runtime requirement, not a compiled-in one: its cost is
        that it must exist on every machine the code runs on, and ADR-0019 says
        which tools a repository may assume. `git` on a hook path and `ssh-keygen`
        on a runner are guaranteed; a language runtime is not.
      - The order does not apply to what is hard to get right and not owned here.
        Cryptography, authentication, TLS, and a parser for a full specification
        somebody else maintains and an adversary can drive — X.509, full YAML, a
        wire protocol — are taken from a maintained implementation, and a
        maintained tool on the host counts. A format this repository defines, or
        a strict subset whose rejection is tighter than the standard's, is written
        here, because the point of it is to reject what the standard would accept.

      **⇢** A runtime feature is the one already shipped: an HTML input type
      before a date-picker package, `Intl` before a formatting library,
      `URLSearchParams` before a parser, an index in Postgres before a scan in
      application code. A host tool is `ssh-keygen -Y sign` before an ed25519
      crate. A format owned here is aval's frontmatter dialect, parsed in-tree
      because a duplicate key must be an error, which YAML's last-wins rule cannot
      give.
  - id: "ci.from-first-commit"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0017"
    source: "The relais repository's first two merges, gated by one workstation and nothing else (2026-09)"
    statement: "A repository has a continuous-integration workflow in its first commit, or in the commit that adds its first check target — whichever is earlier. A repository scaffolded with a build file and no workflow is not finished being scaffolded."
  - id: "ci.gates-the-merge"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0017"
    source: "The relais repository's first two merges, gated by one workstation and nothing else (2026-09)"
    statement: 'A pull request merges when its CI is green, as a separate step after reading the result. It does not merge on the local gate alone, and it is never set to merge automatically before CI has run: without required-status branch protection, "auto-merge" merges immediately. A step that CI skipped on a verified attestation (ADR-0024) is part of a green run, not a merge on the local gate.'
  - id: "ci.laptop-attests-no-artifact"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0024"
    source: "The fleet's attested CI skips, measured on Forgejo (2026-08-20 to 2026-09-30), and the plan review of attested whole-tree lint"
    statement: "A laptop may attest checks and tests. A step that produces what ships (an image, a published bundle, a release asset) is never skipped. Build-as-check and end-to-end runs are attested only by CI itself, for its own platform."
  - id: "ci.mirrors-the-local-check"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0017"
    source: "The relais repository's first two merges, gated by one workstation and nothing else (2026-09)"
    statement: "The workflow runs the repository's own check target — `make check` or its equivalent — with the same commands and flags, so a red job is reproducible on the workstation without pushing. A command that CI runs and the Makefile does not is a defect in the Makefile."
  - id: "ci.platform-bound-checks-stay-strict"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0024"
    source: "The fleet's attested CI skips, measured on Forgejo (2026-08-20 to 2026-09-30), and the plan review of attested whole-tree lint"
    statement: "Formatting and lint gates may be accepted from any platform (the attest action's `anywhere`). Clippy, `go vet`, a typecheck under `cfg`, and any check that compiles or executes the product are accepted only from the platform they would run on in CI."
  - id: "ci.proves-what-only-a-clean-machine-can"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0017"
    source: "The relais repository's first two merges, gated by one workstation and nothing else (2026-09)"
    statement: "Beyond mirroring the local check, a workflow may prove what the workstation cannot: the declared minimum toolchain actually compiling, a platform the workstation is not, a dependency advisory that appeared with nobody pushing. Those jobs are additions, and the advisory one is non-blocking — an advisory is information about a dependency, not a defect in the change."
  - id: "ci.skip-needs-the-same-command"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0024"
    source: "The fleet's attested CI skips, measured on Forgejo (2026-08-20 to 2026-09-30), and the plan review of attested whole-tree lint"
    statement: "A CI step is skipped only on a verified attestation of a gate that ran the byte-identical command, resolved to the same tool version, over the exact tree CI checked out. The declaration and the workflow step are kept identical by a check, never by hand. A step whose command drifted from its gate runs. The attested test skips that predate this rule are a listed, time-limited exception (ADR-0024, Consequences)."
  - id: "ci.system-follows-visibility"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0017"
    source: "The relais repository's first two merges, gated by one workstation and nothing else (2026-09)"
    statement: "An open-source repository published on GitHub runs its checks on GitHub Actions. A private repository hosted on `git.daddyshome.fr` runs them on Forgejo Actions. A repository that changes visibility moves its workflow in the same change."
  - id: "ci.written-for-its-runner"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0017"
    source: "The relais repository's first two merges, gated by one workstation and nothing else (2026-09)"
    statement: "A Forgejo workflow is written to what Forgejo Actions runs, not copied from a GitHub one: `workflow_run` never fires there, there is no artifacts API, and a runner queue that fills kills jobs already running. A workflow that fails for a reason the platform documents was never a workflow for that platform."
  - id: "cli.analytics.no-phone-home"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "A program sends no usage data anywhere without the person's explicit, opt-in consent. Where analytics exist, what is collected, why, how it is anonymized and how long it is kept are stated before consent is asked. Prefer the alternatives clig.dev lists — instrumented web docs, download counts, and talking to the people who use the tool — over instrumenting the tool itself."
  - id: "cli.args.confirm-danger"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "A destructive action asks for confirmation proportionate to its danger, and the non-interactive path to it is an explicit flag. Mild danger (deleting a file the caller named) may need no prompt. Moderate danger (deleting a directory, a remote resource, a bulk change) prompts `y/N` on a terminal, and elsewhere requires `-f/--force` or `--yes`. Severe danger (deleting a whole environment or repository) requires typing its name, or a `--confirm=<name>` flag off a terminal. A `-n/--dry-run` shows what would happen without doing it, wherever the action is worth confirming."
  - id: "cli.args.flags-over-positionals"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Prefer flags to positional arguments: flags are self-describing, can be reordered, and can be added later without breaking a call. Positional arguments are right for one kind of thing, possibly repeated — `rm a b c` — and wrong for two different things: a command with two positionals of different meaning is probably doing something wrong, and the second one should be a flag."
  - id: "cli.args.long-form-always"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Every flag has a long form. One-letter forms are reserved for the flags people type often, so the short namespace is not exhausted on flags nobody remembers; a script should always be able to spell a flag out in full."
  - id: "cli.args.never-require-a-prompt"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Every value a program would prompt for can also be given as a flag or argument, so no invocation needs a terminal to complete. When stdin is not a terminal, or `--no-input` is passed, a program never prompts: if a required value is missing it fails with exit 2 and an error naming the flag that supplies it. A prompt that waits on a pipe hangs a CI job until its timeout."
  - id: "cli.args.no-secrets-in-flags"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "A secret never arrives as a flag value or positional argument: argv is visible to every user through `ps` and is saved in shell history. Secrets are read from a file named by a flag (`--password-file`, `--token-file`), from standard input, or through a prompt that does not echo. There is no `--password` flag."
  - id: "cli.args.optional-value-none"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: 'A long flag that takes an optional value accepts the literal word `none` to mean "explicitly nothing" (`--editor=none`), so the absence of a value is expressible and distinct from the default. Short options never take optional values (`cli.args.posix-syntax`).'
  - id: "cli.args.order-independent"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Flags are accepted before or after subcommands and operands wherever that is unambiguous — `tool --json list` and `tool list --json` do the same thing — because people append a flag to the previous command line. Global flags are defined once and valid at every level. **⇢ Rust:** clap `global = true`. **⇢ Go:** cobra persistent flags."
  - id: "cli.args.posix-syntax"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Options follow the POSIX Utility Syntax Guidelines, extended with GNU long options. A short option is `-` and one alphanumeric character; short options without arguments may be bundled (`-abc`); a short option's argument may be the next word or attached (`-o file`, `-ofile`) and is never optional. A long option is `--` and a lowercase, dash-separated name, taking a value as `--name value` or `--name=value`. `--` ends option processing, so a following `-x` is an operand. `-` as a file operand means standard input, or standard output where the operand names an output. An argument that looks like an option is treated as one. Options may appear after operands (GNU permutation); the last occurrence of a repeated single-valued option wins. Multiple values in one option argument are comma-separated."
  - id: "cli.args.right-default"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "The default behaviour is what most callers want. A flag that nearly everyone passes is a default the tool got wrong."
  - id: "cli.args.standard-names"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Where a flag has a conventional name, that name is used and not repurposed: `-a/--all`, `-d/--debug`, `-f/--force`, `--json`, `-h/--help`, `-n/--dry-run`, `--no-input`, `-o/--output`, `-p/--port`, `-q/--quiet`, `-u/--user`, `--version`, and `-v/--verbose`. clig.dev leaves `-v` open between verbose and version; this fleet takes **verbose**, and version is `--version` (optionally `-V`)."
  - id: "cli.basics.exit-status"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "A program exits 0 when it did what was asked and non-zero when it did not. An invalid invocation — unknown flag, missing argument, malformed value — exits **2**. Other failures use codes from 1 to 125, each distinct wherever a caller has to act differently, and documented in one table. A program never chooses 126, 127 or anything above 128 itself: POSIX gives 126 and 127 to a command that could not run and 128+n to one killed by signal n, so a program interrupted by SIGINT that exits on its own terms exits 130."
    body: |
      **⇢ Rust:** return `std::process::ExitCode` from `main`, mapped from one
      exhaustive `match` over the error type — the table lives in code once, as
      relais does. **⇢ Go:** one `os.Exit(code)` in `main`, nowhere else; a
      library package never exits. **⇢ TypeScript:** set `process.exitCode` and
      return, rather than `process.exit()` mid-flight, which cuts off pending
      stdout writes. BSD `sysexits.h` (64 for usage) is not the fleet's
      vocabulary: 2 for usage is what aval and relais already use, and what
      `getopt`-era utilities do.
  - id: "cli.basics.parse-with-a-library"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Arguments are parsed with the language's standard argument library, never by hand over `argv`, so that help, bundling, `--`, `=` values and error messages behave the same way in every tool. **⇢ Rust:** `clap` with derive. **⇢ Go:** `spf13/pflag` (or `cobra`, which uses it) — not the standard `flag` package, which treats `-name` and `--name` alike and cannot bundle short options, so it cannot meet `cli.args.posix-syntax`. **⇢ TypeScript:** `node:util` `parseArgs` or `commander`."
  - id: "cli.basics.stdout-is-data-stderr-is-talk"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: 'The output a caller asked for goes to standard output; everything else — progress, warnings, errors, prompts, "done" messages, log lines — goes to standard error. A command whose stdout is piped into another program must emit nothing on it that the next program would have to strip.'
    body: |
      This is what makes `tool | jq`, `$(tool)` and `tool > file` work at all. A
      progress line printed to stdout does not look wrong in a terminal and
      corrupts every pipe. Do not treat stderr as a log file either: by default it
      carries messages for the person, not timestamped levels — log formatting is
      for `--debug` or a log destination (see `cli.output.no-log-noise`).
  - id: "cli.compat.interface-is-public"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "A command's flag and subcommand names, its exit codes and its `--json` fields are public interface under ADR-0012: removing, renaming or re-meaning any of them is a major version, adding one is a minor version. Changes are additive wherever they can be; a non-additive one is warned about, in the tool's output, at least one minor release before it lands, with the replacement named. Changing human-readable output is not a breaking change — which is why scripts are pointed at `--json` or `--plain`."
  - id: "cli.compat.no-catch-all-no-time-bomb"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "There is no catch-all subcommand that treats an unknown first word as an implied default command (`tool foo` meaning `tool run foo`): it forbids ever adding a subcommand named `foo`. And the program does not depend on an external service that might disappear — a hard-coded URL, an API with no stability promise — for functions that could work without it; a tool that stops working when a server it did not need goes away is a time bomb."
  - id: "cli.config.ask-before-foreign-edits"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "A program modifies configuration that is not its own — a shell rc file, a git config, another tool's settings — only with the person's consent, and says exactly what it changed. Prefer writing its own file that the other configuration includes, over editing someone else's in place."
  - id: "cli.config.by-volatility"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Where configuration lives follows how often it changes: values that vary per invocation are flags; values stable within a machine or session but varying between projects are flags and environment variables; values stable within a project are a version-controlled file in the project. User and system configuration follows the XDG Base Directory specification (`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, default `~/.config/<tool>/`), not a dotfile in `$HOME`."
  - id: "cli.config.precedence"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "When the same setting comes from several sources, precedence is fixed and documented, highest first: flags, then environment variables, then the project's configuration file, then the user's, then the system's. A value from a lower source never overrides a higher one."
  - id: "cli.dist.single-binary-easy-uninstall"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "A tool is distributed as a single binary where the language allows it, and is as easy to uninstall as to install: the uninstall instructions sit directly below the install instructions, and name every file the tool writes outside its binary."
  - id: "cli.docs.web-and-terminal"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "A tool has documentation on the web (the repository README at minimum, searchable and linkable) and in the terminal (`--help`, and a `help <topic>` subcommand where topics exceed a screen). Man pages are worth generating when the parser can emit them — **⇢ Rust:** `clap_mangen` — and are not required; if provided, `tool help <sub>` and `man tool-<sub>` say the same thing."
  - id: "cli.env.honour-the-standard-variables"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "A program honours the general-purpose variables where they apply: `NO_COLOR` and `FORCE_COLOR` for colour, `DEBUG` for verbose output, `EDITOR` for editing, `HTTP_PROXY`, `HTTPS_PROXY`, `ALL_PROXY` and `NO_PROXY` for network access, `SHELL` to run a shell, `TERM`, `TERMINFO` and `TERMCAP` for terminal capabilities, `TMPDIR` for temporary files, `HOME` and the XDG variables for configuration, `PAGER` for paging, and `LINES` and `COLUMNS` for screen size."
  - id: "cli.env.naming-and-use"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Environment variables carry behaviour that varies with context, not per invocation. A tool's own variables are named `<TOOL>_<NAME>`, uppercase letters, digits and underscores only, not starting with a digit, with single-line values, and never commandeer a widely used name. `.env` files may be read for per-directory context where the tool is a development tool; they are not a substitute for a proper configuration file."
  - id: "cli.env.no-secrets-in-env"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "A tool does not read secrets from environment variables by default: environment is inherited by every child process, dumped by debug output, and often logged. Prefer a file or stdin (`cli.args.no-secrets-in-flags`). Where the platform delivers secrets only as environment — a Kubernetes Secret mapped to env, a CI secret — accepting one is the documented exception, the variable is read once and removed from the environment passed to children, and it is never echoed."
  - id: "cli.errors.rewritten-for-humans"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Expected errors are caught and rewritten to say what went wrong and what to do about it — \"can't write to `out.txt`: permission denied; try `chmod +w out.txt`\" — not surfaced as the library's message or a raw OS error code. Signal-to-noise is the measure: related errors are grouped under one header, not repeated per item, and the most important line comes last, where the eye lands in a terminal (or first, if the output is long enough to scroll — pick for where the person will look). Red is reserved for the error itself."
  - id: "cli.errors.unexpected-errors-are-reportable"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "An unexpected error — a bug — prints what the person needs to report it: the version, the invocation, and a traceback or error chain, either on stderr or written to a file whose path is printed. A link that pre-fills an issue with that information is better still. **⇢ Rust:** a `std::panic::set_hook` that prints the version and an issue URL; `anyhow` or `eyre` for the chain. **⇢ Go:** `%+v` on the wrapped error behind `--debug`."
  - id: "cli.help.concise-by-default"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Run with no arguments when arguments are required, a program prints concise help — what it does, one or two examples, the most common flags, and how to get full help — to stderr, and exits 2. It does not dump the full reference, and it does not start doing something. If the program reads standard input and stdin is a terminal, it prints this help immediately rather than hanging silently waiting for input the person does not know it wants."
  - id: "cli.help.help-and-version"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "`-h` and `--help` print full help, and `--version` prints the version, to standard output, and exit 0. Both are honoured even when other flags or arguments are present and invalid: `tool --bogus --help` prints help, it does not fail on `--bogus`. `-h` means help and nothing else. `-V` may mirror `--version`; `-v` never means version (see `cli.args.standard-names`). A subcommand answers `tool sub --help` with that subcommand's help, and `tool help sub` works too wherever there are subcommands."
  - id: "cli.help.lead-with-examples"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Help text leads with examples of real use, and lists the most common flags and subcommands first, not alphabetically. When the examples outgrow the help screen, they move to a cheat-sheet page or the web docs and help links there. Help uses formatting — bold section headings, aligned columns — that degrades to plain text off a terminal (`cli.output.color-with-intent`)."
  - id: "cli.help.suggest-corrections"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: 'When the invocation is wrong and the intended one can be guessed — a misspelled subcommand, a flag from the wrong subcommand — the error names the likely correction ("did you mean `resolve`?"). It suggests; it never runs the guess, because a guess acted on is a state change the caller did not ask for. **⇢ Rust:** clap''s `suggestions` feature does this.'
  - id: "cli.help.support-path"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Top-level help ends with where to report a problem (the repository's issue tracker) and a link to the web version of the documentation."
  - id: "cli.interactive.tty-only-and-escapable"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Prompts, pickers and other interactive elements appear only when stdin is a terminal (`cli.args.never-require-a-prompt`). A password prompt does not echo. Every interactive state has an obvious way out — a person is never trapped in a prompt, pager or editor without knowing how to leave it — and Ctrl-C keeps working throughout, including during a prompt."
  - id: "cli.naming.short-memorable-lowercase"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "A tool's name is a simple, memorable word, not so generic it collides with something already on PATH, short, easy to type, and lowercase letters with dashes only where needed (POSIX guideline 2 is relaxed for the dash). Subcommand names follow the same rule."
  - id: "cli.output.boundary-crossing-is-explicit"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Actions that cross the program's boundary — writing a file the caller did not name, calling a network service, touching a remote repository — are explicit, not side effects of a local command. Where a tool must reach out implicitly (a freshness check, an update notice), it says so in its docs, bounds the time it spends, and a flag or environment variable turns it off."
  - id: "cli.output.brief-success-state-changes-said"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "On success a command says little — often nothing, per the Unix tradition, but a person with no output cannot tell success from a hang, so a short confirmation on stderr is usually right, and `-q`/`--quiet` silences it. When a command changes state, it says what changed, in terms the person can check. It makes the current state easy to see (a `status` or `list` subcommand), and after a state change suggests the natural next command."
  - id: "cli.output.color-with-intent"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Colour marks meaning — red for an error, yellow for a warning, a highlight on the one thing to read — never decoration, and never as the only carrier of that meaning. Symbols and emoji are used where they make a line faster to scan (✔, ✘, →), sparingly, and never where a script would have to match them. Dense, aligned output (a table, a tree) is preferred over prose where the information is a structure."
  - id: "cli.output.human-first-machine-available"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Default output is written for a person: clear, brief, and readable without knowing the program's internals. Where a caller would plausibly parse the output, the program also offers a machine format, without degrading the human one to get it. Detect a terminal to decide formatting, not content: the same command must not print different *information* to a pipe. **⇢ Rust:** `std::io::IsTerminal`. **⇢ Go:** `golang.org/x/term.IsTerminal`. **⇢ TypeScript:** `process.stdout.isTTY`."
  - id: "cli.output.json-flag"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "A command whose output a program may consume supports `--json`, which prints well-formed JSON to stdout and nothing else there. Its field names and types are public interface: adding a field is a minor change, removing or re-meaning one is a major change under ADR-0012. Errors under `--json` still go to stderr and still set the exit status; a caller never has to parse stdout to learn that the command failed."
  - id: "cli.output.no-animation-off-terminal"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Spinners, progress bars and redrawn lines appear only when the stream they are written to is an interactive terminal. Off a terminal, progress is either absent or a plain line per step, so a CI log is not a wall of carriage returns."
  - id: "cli.output.no-color-off-terminal"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "A program emits no colour, cursor movement or other escape sequence when stdout (or stderr, for what goes there) is not a terminal, when `NO_COLOR` is set to any non-empty value, when `TERM=dumb`, or when `--no-color` is passed. A tool-specific `<TOOL>_NO_COLOR` variable is honoured too if the tool defines one. `FORCE_COLOR` may force colour on for a caller that wants it through a pipe. Escape codes in a CI log or a captured string are noise the caller has to strip."
  - id: "cli.output.no-log-noise"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "By default, a program prints nothing only its authors understand — no internal identifiers, no log levels (`INFO`, `DEBUG`), no timestamps, no stack frames. Those appear under `-d`/`--debug` or `-v`/`--verbose`."
  - id: "cli.output.page-long-text"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Output long enough to scroll off a screen — full help, long diffs, large listings — goes through a pager when stdout is a terminal: `$PAGER`, else `less -FIRX` (quit if it fits one screen, case-insensitive search, pass colour through, leave the screen as it was). Never page when stdout is not a terminal."
  - id: "cli.output.plain-flag"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Where human formatting — wrapped cells, multi-line records, aligned tables — breaks line-oriented tools like `grep` and `awk`, a `--plain` flag prints one record per line with no decoration."
  - id: "cli.robust.recoverable-crash-only"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "A program interrupted at any moment — Ctrl-C, a kill, a lost connection — can simply be run again and continue or complete, rather than leaving state that has to be repaired by hand. Prefer crash-only design: little or no cleanup on exit, with the next run tidying up whatever was left, so an abrupt exit and a normal one are equally safe."
  - id: "cli.robust.responsive"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Responsive matters more than fast: a program prints something within about 100 ms, before any network call, so the person knows it started. Work that takes long shows progress (on a terminal, per `cli.output.no-animation-off-terminal`), and independent work runs in parallel where the output stays readable — interleaved progress bars need a library that knows how to draw several."
  - id: "cli.robust.timeouts"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Every network call and wait has a timeout, with a sensible default and a flag to change it. A tool invoked by a hook or CI never waits forever on a host that stopped answering."
  - id: "cli.robust.validate-early"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Input is validated before any work begins, so an invalid invocation fails fast with exit 2 and changes nothing. People will misuse the program — pass it wrong types, huge inputs, the wrong file — and every such case ends in a clear error, never a panic or a half-applied change."
  - id: "cli.signals.ctrl-c-exits"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "On SIGINT a program stops as soon as possible. It says something immediately if it has cleanup to do, bounds that cleanup with a timeout, and a second Ctrl-C during cleanup skips the rest of it; what a second Ctrl-C will do is printed when the first one arrives, if it matters. A program that swallows Ctrl-C and keeps going is one a person has to kill from another terminal. Exit status after SIGINT follows `cli.basics.exit-status` (130)."
  - id: "cli.subcommands.consistent"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0021"
    source: "Command Line Interface Guidelines (clig.dev), POSIX.1-2024 XBD 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines and XCU 2.8.2 exit status, GNU long-option convention"
    applies:
      - "cli"
    statement: "Subcommands share flag names, output formats and vocabulary: a flag that means something in one subcommand means the same in all of them. Each tool picks `noun verb` or `verb noun` ordering and keeps it at every level of nesting. Two subcommands never have names that are easily confused (`update` and `upgrade`), and subcommands are matched exactly — no implicit prefix abbreviation, because an abbreviation accepted today is a name that can never be added tomorrow. Explicit aliases are fine."
  - id: "comments.accurate-or-deleted"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "A comment is kept accurate or deleted: no obsolete, redundant, misleading or commented-out content, no change journals, no author attribution, no closing-brace markers, no decorative banners."
    body: |
      - Commented-out code is deleted; version control has it.
      - No change journals in source files; version control does that.
      - No author attribution inside files; no irrelevant historical stories.
      - No decorative separators or banners; no closing-brace comments — shorten
        the structure instead.
      - No vague, poorly written, or HTML-heavy comments.
      - No comment far from the code it describes; the relationship between a
        comment and its code is obvious.
      - No compulsory comments added only to satisfy a rule. **⇢** No doc
        comment on every private function to appease a linter; no rustdoc, godoc
        or TSDoc noise on unexported items for completeness.
  - id: "comments.code-first"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "A comment never compensates for unclear code: the idea is expressed first through names, types and extracted functions, and a well-named short function beats a comment explaining a block."
    body: |
      A comment may explain *why* a decision exists; it does not narrate what
      obvious code does. Appropriate comments: legal or licensing text; the intent
      behind a non-obvious decision; a warning about consequences; clarification
      where an external API cannot be made clearer; brief amplification of
      something whose importance could be missed; actionable `TODO`s; documentation
      of a public API. **⇢** Public-API documentation is rustdoc, godoc and TSDoc
      on exported items, and it is expected there.
  - id: "components.acyclic-dependencies"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "There are no cycles in the component dependency graph."
    body: |
      A cycle makes a set of components one big component for release and test
      purposes. Break a cycle with dependency inversion or by extracting the
      shared piece into a new component. **⇢** Cargo and Go refuse cyclic crate
      and module dependencies outright; the rule bites in npm workspaces and
      between modules inside a single crate, where nothing refuses it.
  - id: "components.common-closure"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "Code that changes for the same reason at the same time is gathered into one component; code that changes for different reasons is separated."
    body: |
      Single Responsibility at component scale.
  - id: "components.common-reuse"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "Users of a component are not forced to depend on things they do not need: code that is not reused together does not share a component."
    body: |
      Interface Segregation at component scale. The three cohesion principles
      pull against each other: release-reuse and common-closure are inclusive,
      common-reuse is exclusive. Early projects lean towards common closure
      (fewer components, easy to change); mature ones towards release-reuse and
      common-reuse (stable, reusable). The tension diagram is the map, and the
      component structure grows and is refactored as the system grows rather than
      being designed top-down.
  - id: "components.release-reuse-equivalence"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "The granule of reuse is the granule of release: what is reused together is versioned and released together, with a version number and release notes."
    body: |
      **⇢** ADR-0012 gives the version scheme; a crate, a Go module or a package
      is the unit that carries it. Components are the units of deployment.
  - id: "components.stable-abstractions"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "A stable component is also abstract, so its stability does not prevent extension; a volatile one is concrete."
    body: |
      The Main Sequence runs from (abstract, stable) to (concrete, unstable). The
      Zone of Pain is stable and concrete — a database schema, a widely used
      utility crate; the Zone of Uselessness is abstract and unstable.
  - id: "components.stable-dependencies"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "Depend in the direction of stability: a component many depend on is hard to change and does not itself depend on something volatile."
    body: |
      Instability = fan-out ÷ (fan-in + fan-out).
  - id: "concurrency.explicit-shutdown"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Shutdown behaviour is designed explicitly; graceful shutdown is a feature, not an afterthought."
    body: |
      **⇢** Context cancellation in Go, fiber interruption in Effect,
      cancellation tokens and drop semantics in Rust.
  - id: "concurrency.limit-shared-state"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Access to shared mutable state is limited, protected, and where possible replaced by immutable data or independent copies; tasks are as independent as possible and locked sections are small."
    body: |
      **⇢** Rust's `Send`/`Sync` and the borrow checker enforce most of this; Go's
      race detector is run in CI; Effect `Ref`/`SynchronizedRef` over ad-hoc
      mutation. Beware dependencies between functions taking the same lock.
      Deadlock conditions are understood and actively prevented.
  - id: "concurrency.only-when-it-pays"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Concurrency is used only where it provides a real benefit, is introduced after the non-concurrent code works, and is kept replaceable, configurable and tunable."
    body: |
      - It improves throughput for certain workloads; it does not make code
        faster by itself.
      - Concurrency policy is separated from application logic. **⇢** Effect
        fibers and `Effect.all` with a concurrency option; Go goroutines wired at
        the edge; Rust `tokio` tasks spawned by the caller, not inside the
        function doing the work.
      - Thread counts and related parameters are tunable; concurrent behaviour
        is replaceable.
      - The platform's concurrency facilities are known and used; common
        execution models (producer–consumer, readers–writers) are understood.
  - id: "concurrency.structured"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "Concurrency is structured: a task's lifetime is bounded by the scope that started it, and no promise, goroutine or task is fired and forgotten."
    body: |
      **Effect:** fibers forked inside a scope, `Effect.all` with a concurrency
      option, interruption propagating; never a bare `Promise` created inside
      an `Effect`. **Rust:** `tokio::task::JoinSet` or scoped tasks; a spawned
      task whose handle is dropped is the smell. **Go:** an `errgroup` or a
      `WaitGroup` plus `context`; a `go func()` with no way to wait or cancel is
      the smell. Concurrency is controlled through the effect system, not
      hidden in a library's promise.
  - id: "concurrency.test-repeatedly"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Concurrent code is tested under different loads, worker counts and platforms, repeatedly, with more workers than processors; an intermittent failure is treated as a possible concurrency failure and is never ignored because a rerun passed."
    body: |
      Code is instrumented to raise the probability of problematic interleavings;
      one successful run proves little.
  - id: "data.law-of-demeter"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "A unit knows only its close collaborators: no navigation chains that reach through abstractions, and an operation is asked of the unit that owns the data rather than performed on its extracted internals."
    body: |
      **⇢** Field-access chains on plain data (`order.customer.address.city`) are
      not this smell; method chains that reach through abstractions
      (`a.getB().getC().doSomething()`) are. Builder and iterator chains are not
      this smell either.
  - id: "data.object-or-structure"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "A type is deliberately either a data structure — data exposed, little behaviour — or an abstraction — behaviour exposed, data hidden — and never a hybrid that exposes its internals while pretending to encapsulate."
    body: |
      **⇢** Under ADR-0010 the fleet mostly writes data structures (structs,
      records, schemas) and functions over them; a public field on a plain data
      type is fine. The smell is a "private" field with a getter and setter that
      together expose it anyway. Implementation details hide behind abstractions;
      a base abstraction never depends on its concrete implementations; never
      expose more functions, fields or interfaces than clients need.
  - id: "data.rows-stay-in-the-adapter"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "A persistence row, a generated API type or an ORM record is a data structure that stays in the adapter that produced it; a gateway maps it to a domain-owned type before a use case or a domain function sees it."
    body: |
      **Resolved:** *Clean Code* says an Active Record is a data structure and
      business behaviour lives elsewhere; *Clean Architecture* says nothing in an
      inner circle knows a database row. Both hold, and the translation between
      them is located here: the repository or gateway implementation (the
      adapter) owns the row type and the mapping, the use case owns the domain
      type it receives. A domain function taking a `sqlx` row, an `@effect/sql`
      result struct or a generated client type as input is the violation.
      Data-transfer objects stay simple when their purpose really is transport.
  - id: "dependencies.explicit"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "Every dependency a function needs is visible in its signature — as an argument, a required service, or a trait bound — and none is reached through a global, a singleton or a service locator."
    body: |
      **Effect:** a service `Tag` in `R`, provided by a `Layer` from `main`
      (ADR-0011's `systems.construction-in-main` is the same rule seen from the
      composition root). **Rust:** a generic bound or a trait object parameter;
      no `lazy_static!` client. **Go:** constructor injection of an interface the
      consuming package declares. Inject clocks, configuration and external
      services the same way; they are dependencies like any other.
  - id: "design-system.a-missing-token-is-added-not-approximated"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0027"
    source: "An inventory of `@duro-app/ui` on 2026-10-06 (about 60 size literals in 25 components, about 60 border widths, no size or border tokens), and a 44 px touch-target literal that passed every review in Duro 4.5.0"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: "When no token fits, add one with the value the design needs. Do not borrow the nearest spacing token as a size."
    body: |
      The 2026-10-06 inventory found `spacing.md` and `spacing.xl` used as
      widths in Tree and Table. A spacing token is a gap between things; a size
      token is the size of a thing. When the spacing scale changes, a borrowed
      width changes with it, and nobody decided that.
  - id: "design-system.accessibility-lives-in-the-component"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0006"
    source: "ADR-0006's decision that the design system is where a missing component is added, and a Duro consumer that hand-worked around the design system's form labels (application-landscape, 2026-09)"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: "Labels that point at their control, names on groups, focus order, roles and states are part of a design-system component's contract, because they are the part that is easy to get subtly wrong and worth solving once (ADR-0006, Why). A consumer that has to add an `id`, an `aria-*` attribute or a wrapper to make a design-system component accessible has found a gap in the component: fix it there, and let every consumer inherit the fix."
  - id: "design-system.every-measure-is-a-token"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0027"
    source: "An inventory of `@duro-app/ui` on 2026-10-06 (about 60 size literals in 25 components, about 60 border widths, no size or border tokens), and a 44 px touch-target literal that passed every review in Duro 4.5.0"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: "In `@duro-app/ui` and in every Duro consumer's styles, a measure is a published Duro token: spacing, radius, size or border width."
    body: |
      A missing token is a gap that goes upstream
      (`design-system.gaps-go-upstream`). It is never a local literal.

      These are the only exemptions:

      - `0`;
      - relative units (`%`, `vh`, `vw`, `fr`, `auto`, `min-content`…);
      - a `calc()` made only of tokens, relative units and unitless multipliers
        (`calc(-1 * focusOffset)`);
      - the 1×1 visually-hidden technique, in the single module
        `packages/ui/src/styles/visually-hidden.css.ts`;
      - SVG `viewBox` path coordinates, which draw a glyph and do not size a box;
      - the intrinsic canvas size of a `Diagram` (its `width` and `height`
        props), because the canvas is the diagram's coordinate space;
      - media queries on the breakpoint scale, because a query cannot read a
        variable;
      - layout positions in artboards (top, left, inset, transform);
      - inline email styles, in `@duro-app/ui-email` and in a consumer's email
        templates, which interpolate raw token values because mail clients cannot
        read CSS variables.

      `duro/no-raw-design-values` is the mechanical form.
  - id: "design-system.gap-found-is-gap-reported"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0006"
    source: "ADR-0006's decision that the design system is where a missing component is added, and a Duro consumer that hand-worked around the design system's form labels (application-landscape, 2026-09)"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: "A gap met while doing something else is not silently worked around and not silently left. Either it is fixed upstream in the same effort, or it is recorded where the design system tracks work, and the consumer's pull request says which — so the next consumer does not rediscover it."
  - id: "design-system.gaps-go-upstream"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0006"
    source: "ADR-0006's decision that the design system is where a missing component is added, and a Duro consumer that hand-worked around the design system's form labels (application-landscape, 2026-09)"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: "When a consumer needs something `@duro-app/ui` does not provide — a component, a variant, a prop, a layout, or a behaviour such as an accessible name — the change is made in the design system, released, and then taken by the consumer. The consumer does not bridge the gap locally: no look-alike component, no raw element standing in for a design-system one, no stylesheet or attribute patched on top to supply what the component should."
    body: |
      If the consumer is several releases behind, upgrading it to the release that
      closes the gap is part of the same work, not a reason to patch locally
      instead.
  - id: "design-system.no-local-substitute"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0006"
    source: "ADR-0006's decision that the design system is where a missing component is added, and a Duro consumer that hand-worked around the design system's form labels (application-landscape, 2026-09)"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: "A consumer takes a component from `@duro-app/ui` whenever the design system has one. It does not write its own version, and it does not drop to raw markup (`<div>` grid, native `<fieldset>`, hand-drawn toggle) to get behaviour the design-system component lacks — that lack is a gap, and a gap goes upstream."
    body: |
      Plain markup around design-system components — the layout of a page,
      domain-specific composition — stays the consumer's, as its own conventions
      say. The line is the component: what the design system would ship, it
      ships.
  - id: "design.four-priorities"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: 'A simple design, in this order: the system passes all its tests; it contains no duplication; it expresses the programmer''s intent; it minimises unnecessary units and functions — and the order means "fewest units" never justifies duplication, unreadability or an untested system.'
    body: |
      - Refactor only while tests are green.
      - Names and structure reveal intent.
      - Unnecessary abstractions are removed.
      - The system is not fragmented into tiny units merely to follow a rule.
      - A simple design that satisfies current requirements is preferred.
  - id: "details.database-is-a-detail"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "The database is an I/O device with a data model; the domain model is not the schema and the schema is not the domain model."
  - id: "details.frameworks-are-details"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "A framework is used, not married: it stays outside the architectural boundary, never enters the entities or use cases, and can be replaced."
    body: |
      A framework author's interests are not yours. **⇢** ADR-0005 chooses a web
      framework and ADR-0001 an effects runtime. Effect is the one deliberate
      exception, adopted as the language of the code rather than a framework
      outside it, and that is a recorded decision rather than a drift.
  - id: "details.let-the-rule-decide"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "Separate the actors, separate the components, draw the boundaries where the use cases and the details meet, and let the Dependency Rule decide every arrow."
    body: |
      The book's case study in one sentence.
  - id: "details.platform-is-a-detail"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "The operating system, the cloud provider and the runtime are details, isolated behind an interface the policy owns."
    body: |
      The book's clean embedded architecture, with its hardware, firmware and
      software layers, is this rule for a different platform.
  - id: "details.web-is-a-detail"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "The web is an I/O device and delivery mechanism; the use cases do not know they are behind HTTP."
    body: |
      **⇢** ADR-0003 chooses the HTTP server for the Effect stack; the choice is a
      detail precisely because the use cases never import it.
  - id: "domain.independent-of-infrastructure"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "Domain logic depends on nothing that is infrastructure: no database, HTTP, framework, filesystem or clock inside it, and no import from the packages that provide them."
    body: |
      This is ADR-0011's `architecture.dependency-rule` read from the domain's
      side, and the two are one rule. **Effect:** the domain module imports
      `effect` and its own types, never `@effect/sql` or `@effect/platform`.
      **Rust:** the domain crate has no I/O dependencies in `Cargo.toml`. **Go:**
      the domain package's import list contains no driver.
  - id: "effects.at-the-boundary"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "Effects are pushed to the boundary of the application: a functional core of calculations, an imperative shell of actions around it, and the shell as thin as the language allows."
    body: |
      **Effect:** external systems are implemented at the edge through
      `Layer`s; the use case composes `Effect`s over pure domain functions; the
      only place that talks to the world is a service implementation. **Rust:**
      the binary crate owns I/O and hands values to library crates that do not.
      **Go:** `main` and the adapter packages do I/O; the domain package imports
      neither `net/http` nor `database/sql`. Grokking Simplicity's stratified
      design: the layers lower down change less and depend on less.
  - id: "effects.describe-then-run"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "The description of a program is separated from its execution: an effect is a value that says what will happen, and running it is a distinct, late step."
    body: |
      **Effect:** an `Effect<A, E, R>` is a description; nothing happens until a
      runtime runs it, which is why it can be composed, retried, timed out and
      tested as a value. **Rust:** a `Future` is a description until polled;
      builders and command values (`enum Command`) that an interpreter executes
      are the same idea. **Go:** has no lazy effect type; the nearest form is a
      plan value (`[]Step`, a `Command` struct) executed by one function, used
      where the plan needs to be inspected or tested before it runs. From
      *Functional Programming in Scala*, chapter 13.
  - id: "effects.no-ambient-access"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "No hidden reads of the clock, randomness, the environment, configuration or global state inside a calculation; each is an explicit input or an explicit service."
    body: |
      **Effect:** `Clock`, `Random`, `Config` are services required in `R`, not
      `Date.now()` and `Math.random()` in the body. **Rust:** pass `now:
      DateTime` or a `Clock` trait; `std::env::var` only in `main` or a config
      loader. **Go:** `time.Now()` and `os.Getenv` only in `main` and adapters;
      a domain function takes `now time.Time`. A function that reads the clock is
      an action, and it cannot be tested without controlling time.
  - id: "errors.explicit-absence"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: 'Absence is a type the caller can see, never a null the caller has to guess about, and never passed in as a way of saying "no value".'
    body: |
      **⇢** Rust and Effect make absence explicit (`Option`, `Option.none()`). Go:
      a zero value plus `error`, or a `bool`, and the doc comment says which.
      TypeScript: `undefined` only under a type that says so, never a bare `null`
      in a signature that does not name it. Prefer an empty collection, an
      explicit result type, an optional value or an error, as appropriate; a
      special-case or null-object implementation simplifies the normal path where
      it fits. No optional parameter that the callee must nil-check to discover
      the caller's intent — use a type that names the case.
  - id: "errors.never-swallowed"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "An error is never silently suppressed."
    body: |
      **⇢** No `_ = err`, no `let _ = fallible()`, no empty `catch`, no
      `.catchAll(() => Effect.void)` without a comment on the same line saying why
      the failure is genuinely irrelevant.
  - id: "errors.precise-contracts"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Contracts and failure behaviour are precise: what a function promises, what it can fail with, and what state it leaves behind are all stated."
  - id: "errors.typed-values"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "A failure is a typed value in the signature — never a numeric or boolean code, never a bare throw — carrying the operation that failed and the entity it failed on, with third-party errors wrapped behind application-owned types."
    body: |
      **⇢** The book says "exceptions over error codes" and "unchecked over
      checked"; both stand for one rule — a failure carries its meaning, cannot be
      ignored by accident, and is handled where recovery is possible rather than
      where the compiler forces it. Rust: `Result<T, E>` with a typed `E`, never a
      sentinel, propagated with `?` and wrapped with context on the way up. Go: an
      `error` value always checked, wrapped with `fmt.Errorf("...: %w", err)`.
      Effect: a typed error channel, `Data.TaggedError` per distinguishable
      outcome. An untyped `throw` in TypeScript is the error-code smell in another
      costume. A panic, a Go `panic`, or a TypeScript `throw` is for a bug, not for
      an expected failure. Error types are defined by what callers need to
      distinguish; the failure path is written early; error-handling scopes are
      focused.
  - id: "failures.expected-vs-defect"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "An expected failure — a case the domain knows about — is a value in the return type; a defect — a bug, a broken invariant — is the only thing that panics, throws or dies."
    body: |
      **Effect:** the typed error channel `E` for expected failures, `Effect.die`
      and defects for the rest; `catchAll` on a defect is hiding a bug. **Rust:**
      `Result<T, E>` for expected, `panic!` and `unwrap` only where the invariant
      is proven. **Go:** an `error` return for expected, `panic` for never. The
      mechanism is ADR-0011's `errors.typed-values`; this rule is the boundary
      between the two kinds, which that rule does not draw.
  - id: "formatting.newspaper-layout"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "A file reads like a newspaper: the title and high-level concepts first, progressively more detail lower down, related things close together."
    body: |
      - Distinct concepts are separated by blank lines; tightly related
        statements stay together.
      - Local variables are declared near first use; loop-control variables live
        inside the loop.
      - Instance variables sit in a predictable place. **⇢** Struct fields are
        declared once at the top of the type, in a consistent order.
      - Dependent functions sit close; conceptually related code sits close even
        without a call relationship; higher-level functions above lower-level
        ones.
      - Lines are reasonably short; whitespace exposes relationships (assignment
        versus operator precedence); no horizontal alignment whose maintenance
        creates noise.
      - Indentation is consistent; real code is never hidden in an unindented
        body; no empty loop or conditional bodies, and when unavoidable they are
        conspicuous.
      - Files are small and focused on one coherent subject.
  - id: "formatting.one-style-by-tool"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "One team style, applied by the formatter, and nothing hand-formatted against it."
    body: |
      **⇢** `rustfmt`, `gofmt`, and the repository's Prettier or Biome config are
      the style. A formatting argument in review is settled by running the tool.
  - id: "functions.command-query-separation"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "A function changes state or answers a question, not both."
  - id: "functions.error-handling-is-one-thing"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Error handling is one responsibility on its own, extracted so it does not obscure the normal path."
    body: |
      **⇢** In Rust, `?` and a named error type; in Go, early return on `err`; in
      Effect, typed errors in the effect's signature and `catchTag` at the edge.
  - id: "functions.few-arguments"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "A function takes no more inputs than it uses; related inputs travel as one value; and no input is hidden in ambient state to lower the count."
    body: |
      **Resolved:** the book says zero arguments is ideal, one common, two
      reasonable, three or more need justification. That ranking assumes methods
      on a receiver holding state. Under functions-over-data (ADR-0010) an
      explicit input is the dependency declaration, and hiding one in a global or
      a closure to make the signature shorter is the actual smell. So:

      - A related group of arguments becomes a parameter object. **⇢** A struct,
        a Go options struct, a TypeScript object parameter with named fields.
      - Argument roles are expressed by names or types.
      - A count of three or more is a prompt to look for a missing type, not a
        violation on its own.
  - id: "functions.no-flag-arguments"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "No boolean flag arguments: a flag announces two functions in one, and two explicitly named functions beat `execute(true)` and `execute(false)`."
    body: |
      Also: no function whose behaviour is selected by a string or integer
      argument (a selector argument). Where the selection is over a closed set,
      it is a sum type handled in one exhaustive match, or two functions.
  - id: "functions.no-hidden-side-effects"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "A function has no hidden side effects: it does not unexpectedly modify global state, its arguments, or unrelated collaborators, and it does not rely on a caller remembering an invocation order."
    body: |
      - No output parameters; return a value or mutate the receiver. **⇢** Rust
        `&mut` out-parameters and Go pointer out-parameters are this smell;
        return a value, a tuple or a struct instead.
      - Temporal requirements are explicit. **⇢** Encode the order in types: a
        builder that only exposes `build()` once required fields are set, an
        Effect that requires the service, a Go constructor that returns the only
        valid handle.
  - id: "functions.polymorphism-over-repeated-switch"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Where type-dependent branching repeats through the system, the variation moves into one dispatch point rather than being re-switched at every call."
    body: |
      **⇢** Under ADR-0010 that dispatch point is a trait, an interface, or a sum
      type with an exhaustive `match` / discriminated union — never an
      inheritance hierarchy. One `switch` on a kind field is fine; the same
      `switch` in five places is the smell.
  - id: "functions.small-and-single-purpose"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "A function is small, does one thing, does it completely, and keeps every statement at the same level of abstraction."
    body: |
      - Blocks inside `if`, `else`, loops and error-handling arms are small.
      - Extract a block into a well-named function when that improves reading.
      - Business rules, low-level parsing and infrastructure do not share a
        function.
      - Complicated conditions are encapsulated in meaningfully named predicates;
        positive conditions over double negatives.
      - Functions are built by successive refinement: write, test, extract,
        rename, reorganise.
      - Unused functions are removed; duplicated implementation is eliminated.

      **Resolved:** this is a heuristic, not a constraint, because *A Philosophy
      of Software Design* argues the opposite (deep functions over many shallow
      ones) with a case the fleet finds partly right. The test is whether the
      reader understands the function without scrolling; a longer function that
      reads top-down in one pass is not a violation.
  - id: "functions.top-down-order"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "A file reads top-down from high-level intention to low-level detail: callers above callees where practical, related functions physically close."
    body: |
      **⇢** Rust and Go allow either order; the rule is about reading, so keep it.
  - id: "functions.total"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "A function is total: it handles every value its input type admits, rather than panicking, throwing or returning a wrong answer for some of them."
    body: |
      Where an input is only sometimes valid, narrow the input type
      (`values.no-primitive-obsession`) or widen the output to say so
      (`Option`, `Result`, `Either`). A `NonEmptyArray` parameter beats an
      `array` with a runtime check. *Domain Modeling Made Functional*, on
      totality.
  - id: "general.catalogue-remainder"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "The Chapter 17 catalogue items not carried by a rule above hold as written: expected behaviour is implemented, abstractions stay at their level, logical dependencies are made physical, and the algorithm is understood rather than patched."
    body: |
      The catalogue's counts by category, so a citation can name an item:
      Comments 5, Environment 2, Functions 4, General 36, Java 3, Names 7,
      Tests 9 — 66 in all.

      - One main language per source file where practical.
      - Behaviour that users can reasonably expect is implemented.
      - Every boundary condition is found and tested.
      - Each abstraction stays at its correct level; no artificial coupling
        between unrelated modules.
      - Logical dependencies are made physical: a module that depends on a fact
        about another asks that module for it rather than assuming it.
      - Configurable values live at a high level, not buried in a low-level
        function.
      - Boundary calculations are encapsulated rather than repeated (`size - 1`).
      - No transitive navigation through object graphs.
      - Structures are not chosen arbitrarily; the reason is visible.
      - The algorithm is understood rather than patched until tests happen to
        pass.
      - Vertical separation: definitions sit close to their use.
      - Consistency: a thing done a certain way is done that way everywhere.
      - Clutter: no unused variables, uncalled functions, or comments that add
        nothing.
      - Intent is not obscured by terse or dense expression.
      - Explanatory variables name intermediate results.
      - Structure over convention: a design that enforces a rule beats a
        convention that asks for it.
      - The three Java-specific items (wildcard imports, constants over enums,
        constants not inherited) translate to: import what is used, by name; a
        fixed set of values is an enum or a union type, never a set of constants;
        a constant is referenced from the module that owns it, not re-exported
        through an unrelated one.
  - id: "general.continuous-cleanliness"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Cleanliness is maintained continuously and never postponed to a rewrite: leave code a little cleaner than you found it, and refactor incrementally while preserving behaviour."
    body: |
      The Boy Scout Rule. A change that touches a function may tidy that function;
      it does not have to tidy the file, and a review does not ask it to.
  - id: "general.heuristics-are-debatable"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "The book is opinionated, Java-centric and from 2008, and its author calls its rules debatable; a reviewer citing a heuristic still owes the argument for why it applies here."
    body: |
      Extremely small functions, "avoid null", one assertion per test and
      conditionals-to-polymorphism are the items most often over-applied. The
      constraints in this file are the fleet's own selection of what is firm; the
      rest is judgement, and this rule says so.
  - id: "general.linters-police-suppressions"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Each repository configures its linters to reject the suppressions that `general.no-disabled-safety` forbids, so the tool checks the rule and review does not have to."
    body: |
      - TypeScript: `linterOptions.reportUnusedDisableDirectives: "error"`;
        `@typescript-eslint/ban-ts-comment`, which allows only
        `@ts-expect-error` with a description; and the
        `@eslint-community/eslint-comments` rules `no-unlimited-disable`,
        `require-description` and `disable-enable-pair`.
      - Python: ruff `PGH003`, `PGH004` and `RUF100`; pyright
        `reportUnnecessaryTypeIgnoreComment = "error"` and
        `enableTypeIgnoreComments = false`, so only the rule-specific
        `# pyright: ignore[rule]` form works.
      - Rust: clippy `allow_attributes` and `allow_attributes_without_reason`, so
        a suppression is `#[expect(lint, reason = "…")]` and fails once the lint
        stops firing.
      - Go: golangci-lint `nolintlint` with `require-explanation`,
        `require-specific` and `allow-unused: false`.

      A linter does not see its own configuration. A rule turned off there is
      caught in review, and by an agent hook where one is installed.
  - id: "general.no-disabled-safety"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Safety mechanisms, failing tests and lint findings are never disabled to make work easier."
    body: |
      **⇢** A finding is fixed in the code that raised it. The type, the test
      and the lint rule are not loosened instead.

      - **A suppression needs a cause outside the repository.** That is a false
        positive in the tool, or a missing or wrong type in a dependency. The
        reason on the same line names the cause: the upstream issue, the stub
        package, the tool bug. "Hard to type", "too complex" and "will fix later"
        are causes inside the repository.
      - **A suppression is as narrow as the tool allows**: one line, one rule
        code, in the form the tool checks — `@ts-expect-error`,
        `#[expect(lint, reason = "…")]`, `# pyright: ignore[rule]`,
        `# noqa: CODE`, `//nolint:linter // reason`. Blanket forms are not
        allowed: `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-nocheck`, a bare `# type: ignore` or
        `# noqa`, a `//nolint` with no linter.
      - **No file-level or configuration-level disable gets a change through.**
        That means no `/* eslint-disable */` over a file, no `# pyright: basic`,
        `# pyright: reportX=false` or `# ruff: noqa` header, and no rule turned off, downgraded or narrowed
        and no strictness lowered in `eslint.config.*`, `tsconfig.json`,
        `pyproject.toml`, `pyrightconfig.json`, `[lints]` in `Cargo.toml` or
        `.golangci.yml`. Loosening a configuration is a decision of its own: its
        own commit, the person's agreement, and the reason in the commit body.
      - **A finding the change cannot fix is reported, not hidden.** An agent
        that cannot fix one stops and says so; it does not suppress it.
      - No `--no-verify`, no skipped or expected-failure test to get a green run.
  - id: "general.no-duplication"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "True duplication — the same knowledge stated twice, which must change together — is removed wherever it appears; accidental duplication, two things that look alike now and serve different actors, is left separate."
    body: |
      **Resolved:** *Clean Code* says remove duplication wherever it appears;
      *Clean Architecture* qualifies it. Two use cases that share a screen shape
      today and will diverge tomorrow are not duplicates, and reuniting them
      couples actors that should stay apart. The test is: if one changed, would
      the other be wrong? Yes, it is duplication. No, it is coincidence.
  - id: "general.no-speculation"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Implement what is needed now; no speculative functionality, no infrastructure for hypothetical future scale."
  - id: "general.one-command-build-and-test"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "The project builds with one simple command and the entire test suite runs with one simple command."
  - id: "general.read-more-than-written"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Code is read far more often than it is written, so it is optimised for the reader: working is necessary, communicating intent is the standard."
    body: |
      - Simplicity and readability make development faster over the life of the
        system, not slower.
      - Prefer straightforward code over clever code.
      - Treat code as writing: structure it for a human audience.
      - First make it work, then make it right, and do not stop after the first
        step.
      - Follow the team's conventions consistently.
  - id: "goal.minimise-lifetime-cost"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "The goal of architecture is to minimise the human effort required to build and maintain the system over its lifetime, keeping as many options open as possible for as long as possible."
    body: |
      - Behaviour is urgent; structure is important. The architect's job is to
        keep structure from being sacrificed to the urgent.
      - A good architecture makes the system easy to understand, develop,
        maintain and deploy.
      - The only way to go fast is to go well.
  - id: "guidance.a-generation-warning-is-an-instruction"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0018"
    source: "A fleet-wide AGENTS.md sweep after the amont 1.37.0 upgrade left a hundred live checkouts dirty (2026-09)"
    statement: "When the gate reports the block behind the installed tool and does not fix it itself, the committer runs the named fix (`amont agents-md`) in the same working tree and stages the result into the same commit. A gate that never blocks is not a gate that may be skipped; it is one that trusts the committer to act."
  - id: "guidance.always-on-is-an-index"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0026"
    source: "ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English (writing rules, not the dictionary); the caveman project's rules and benchmarks (github.com/JuliusBrussee/caveman, 2026-10); a measured 31,450-byte session hook"
    statement: "Text that a repository puts into every agent session is an index of one line per item."
    body: |
      Each line is the item's id and what it is about. Hook output, `CLAUDE.md`
      and `AGENTS.md` link to detail; they do not contain it. The detail is fetched
      when a task needs it: `aval rule <id>`, a linked file, a skill. A block
      that every session pays for and few sessions use goes behind a link.
  - id: "guidance.gate-output-is-failures"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0026"
    source: "ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English (writing rules, not the dictionary); the caveman project's rules and benchmarks (github.com/JuliusBrussee/caveman, 2026-10); a measured 31,450-byte session hook"
    statement: "A check that a git hook or CI runs prints each failure in full and its success in one line. Configure the test reporter and the linter for this: failures with their message, file and line; for a pass, a count and a duration. An agent reads every byte that a gate prints, and a passing suite of a thousand lines tells it one fact. Full output stays available behind a flag or in a log file that the one line names."
  - id: "guidance.generated-block-rides-the-change"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0018"
    source: "A fleet-wide AGENTS.md sweep after the amont 1.37.0 upgrade left a hundred live checkouts dirty (2026-09)"
    statement: "A tool-generated block in a tracked file is regenerated by the pre-commit gate of the tool that owns it and staged into the commit being made, so the pull request that needed the change carries the refreshed block. It is not refreshed by hand as a separate act, not written into a live checkout by a fleet-wide sweep, and never committed to `main` outside a pull request."
  - id: "guidance.no-generated-file-travels-alone"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0018"
    source: "A fleet-wide AGENTS.md sweep after the amont 1.37.0 upgrade left a hundred live checkouts dirty (2026-09)"
    statement: "A generated file changes in the commit that changed its source, next to the change that made it necessary — not in a chore pull request of its own per repository, and not in a sweep that touches every repository at once. Where a gate exists to regenerate it, the gate does it; where none exists, the author does, in the same commit."
  - id: "handoff.every-measure-a-token"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0027"
    source: "Measured drift on duro-app's approval-gates screen (2026-09), and the design system's own token and recipe contract"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: "An artboard references a design value — colour, spacing, radius, size, border width, type, shadow, duration, easing — only through a published Duro token name (`var(--duro-color-accent)`, `var(--duro-size-touch-target)`, `var(--duro-border-hairline)`); never a hex, an `rgb()`, a pixel or a millisecond."
    body: |
      The exemptions are the list in `design-system.every-measure-is-a-token`,
      and only that list. Layout positions are one of them: where an element sits
      is the sketch.

      `duro mockup check` is the mechanical form; an artboard that fails it is not
      proposed.
  - id: "handoff.fresh-directions-trigger"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0027"
    source: "Measured drift on duro-app's approval-gates screen (2026-09), and the design system's own token and recipe contract"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: "Fresh directions are drawn for a new screen, or for a material choice in layout, hierarchy or interaction. They are drawn with the `duro mockup` CLI, through the `/duro-mockup` skill, as two to four directions the person picks from. A fix that restores an existing design reuses the picked artboard instead. Any other interface change needs no mockup: it goes through browser verification and the person's preview (ADR-0028, `work.preview-unless-planned-evidence`)."
    body: |
      This rule only says *when* a mockup is drawn and *with what*. What an
      artboard contains is `handoff.every-measure-a-token` and `handoff.name-the-component`,
      what binds is `handoff.what-binds`, and the proof is
      `handoff.prove-fidelity`. A picked artboard may be committed with the plan
      on the task branch, ahead of the implementation it binds.
  - id: "handoff.name-the-component"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0027"
    source: "Measured drift on duro-app's approval-gates screen (2026-09), and the design system's own token and recipe contract"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: 'Every element in an artboard that a person can act on — however it is drawn, a `<div>` with a pointer cursor as much as a `<button>` — carries `data-duro="<Component>"` or `data-duro="<Component>.<Part>"` naming the `@duro-app/ui` component that will implement it. Notes for the implementer follow the name (`data-duro="Button variant=primary"`).'
    body: |
      An artboard with no component map at all is not a proposal: it cannot be
      implemented from without the translation this record exists to remove.
  - id: "handoff.prove-fidelity"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0027"
    source: "Measured drift on duro-app's approval-gates screen (2026-09), and the design system's own token and recipe contract"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: "A pull request that implements a picked direction records the canvas URL and the direction, commits the artboards under the repository's mockups directory (one folder per screen), and shows the picked artboard beside a screenshot of the built route taken in a real browser at the artboard's width and theme. A pull request that touches no screen says so explicitly."
    body: |
      Where the screenshot and the artboard disagree on hierarchy or states, that
      is a finding to fix before review, not a caption to write.
  - id: "handoff.recipe-before-grid"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0027"
    source: "Measured drift on duro-app's approval-gates screen (2026-09), and the design system's own token and recipe contract"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: "A new screen starts from the closest design-system recipe (`duro list recipes`) and edits it. A page layout written from a bare `Grid` or a hand-rolled `gridTemplateColumns` with its own media query needs a stated reason in the pull request — most often that the recipe does not exist yet, in which case adding it to the design system is the better change."
  - id: "handoff.what-binds"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0027"
    source: "Measured drift on duro-app's approval-gates screen (2026-09), and the design system's own token and recipe contract"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: "Once a direction is picked, layout, hierarchy, states, the component map and the token names bind the implementation; sizes and border widths bind through their tokens. The artboard's positions do not, and neither does any literal the canvas editor wrote back over a token after the pick."
    body: |
      The heuristic is where judgement lives: if an artboard's column is
      `var(--duro-size-grid-col-sm)` wide and `Grid layout="split"` lets it grow,
      the grid wins without discussion; if the artboard shows three states and the screen ships
      two, that is a departure and needs a reason.
  - id: "i18n.cite-the-english-entry"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0025"
    source: "ADR-0025's decision that user-facing text comes from the i18n catalog, its English entry being the reference value"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: 'Wherever a string has to be named outside the lookup that renders it — a test selector or assertion, a fixture, a story, a doc example — the English entry is looked up, not retyped: `getByRole("button", { name: t("entity.add.submit") })`, not `"Add"`. Tests run with the catalog pinned to English (`lng: "en"` or the framework''s equivalent). Only a test whose subject is the localization itself reads another locale''s entry, through the lookup the same way. Nothing matches several languages by hand (`/Name|Nom/`).'
    body: |
      The lookup is a function given the key — the same `t` the app renders
      through, or a test helper over it — never a walk of the catalog object
      (`en.entity.add.submit`). The function is what applies interpolation,
      plurals and namespaces, so only it returns what the person reads. It
      throws on a missing entry: `t("entity.add.sumbit")` must fail, not return
      `undefined` or the key — a selector given `name: undefined` matches any
      name.
  - id: "i18n.fix-literals-when-touched"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0025"
    source: "ADR-0025's decision that user-facing text comes from the i18n catalog, its English entry being the reference value"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: "Existing hardcoded copy, `/A|B/` alternations and key assertions are fixed when the code or test around them changes, not in a sweep nobody asked for."
  - id: "i18n.literal-only-when-needed"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0025"
    source: "ADR-0025's decision that user-facing text comes from the i18n catalog, its English entry being the reference value"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: "A literal is written only where the catalog cannot be the source: the repository has no catalog, the text is deliberately not localized (a log line, a protocol token, a product or brand name, output of a command line that has no catalog), it is test data a person would type rather than text the interface shows, or it is the catalog entry itself, written in the change that adds it. Each such literal is a choice a reviewer can see; one that turns out to be user-facing copy goes to the catalog."
  - id: "i18n.never-the-key"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0025"
    source: "ADR-0025's decision that user-facing text comes from the i18n catalog, its English entry being the reference value"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: "A key is an identifier, never text. It is not a selector, not an expected value in an assertion, and not what the interface shows when a translation is missing — the fallback is the English entry, and in tests a missing entry fails. Testing against keys (i18next `cimode` and similar) proves a lookup happened, not what the person reads."
  - id: "i18n.text-comes-from-the-catalog"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0025"
    source: "ADR-0025's decision that user-facing text comes from the i18n catalog, its English entry being the reference value"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: "When the catalog has a string, code renders it through the catalog lookup. No component, page, dialog, toast, `aria-label`, `title` or `alt` writes the text as a literal — not in English, not in any other language. A new string is added to the catalog (English entry at least) in the same change that first renders it."
  - id: "names.context-from-structure"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Context comes from the module, type and function around a name, not from repeating it inside every name; no redundant application prefix on every type."
  - id: "names.one-word-per-concept"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "One word for one concept, used everywhere, and never the same word for two unrelated concepts."
    body: |
      - Choose `fetch`, `get` or `retrieve`, and use it everywhere.
      - Domain vocabulary for business concepts; established technical vocabulary
        for implementation concepts (`Factory`, `Repository`, `Adapter`,
        `Visitor`). **⇢** Also the language's own idioms: `Builder`, `Iterator`,
        `Layer`, `Handler`.
  - id: "names.parts-of-speech"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "A type name is a noun, a function name is a verb, a boolean reads as a predicate, and an accessor follows the language's convention."
    body: |
      - Type names are nouns or noun phrases. **⇢** Structs, enums, traits,
        interfaces, type aliases and Go types alike.
      - No vague type names such as `Manager`, `Processor`, `Data`, `Info`
        unless they genuinely express the concept.
      - Function names are verbs or verb phrases and say what the function
        actually does; an unexpected side effect appears in the name.
      - Booleans read as predicates: `isValid`, `hasAccess`, `canRetry`.
      - Accessors follow the language's convention. **⇢** Rust and Go: the field
        name for a getter (`len()`, `name()`), `set_x`/`SetX` for a setter,
        `is_x`/`IsX` for a predicate; TypeScript: `get`/`set`/`is` per the
        surrounding code.
  - id: "names.pronounceable-and-searchable"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Names are pronounceable and searchable, and no two differ only by an insignificant word, a number, or a visual near-match."
    body: |
      - Not `customer`, `customerInfo`, `customerData`, `customer1`.
      - No misleading names: nothing called `accountList` that is not a list.
      - No jokes, cultural references or cute names.
  - id: "names.rename-aggressively"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: 'Rename as soon as a better understanding emerges; a rename is a legitimate change on its own, and "what is `res` here" in review is a defect report.'
  - id: "names.reveal-intent"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "A name states what the thing is in the vocabulary of the domain, so a reader never decodes an abbreviation, guesses at a placeholder, or infers a type from a prefix."
    body: |
      - A variable describes what it represents, not its type.
      - No unexplained single-letter names such as `d`, `x`, `n`. A very short
        name such as `i` is acceptable only in a tiny, conventional scope. **⇢**
        Go's short receiver names and a Rust closure parameter over a named
        collection (`positions.iter().map(|p| ...)`) are this exception, not a
        licence.
      - The larger the scope, the longer and more descriptive the name.
      - Name after the concept: `elapsedTimeInDays`, not `d`.
      - No placeholder names: `data`, `result`, `value`, `item`, `tmp`, `obj` say
        nothing the type does not already say.
      - No abbreviations unless the abbreviation *is* the domain word (`isin`,
        `mtu`, `url`, `id`); `cfg`, `ctx`, `mgr`, `svc` are not domain words.
      - No types encoded in names, no Hungarian notation, no member prefixes such
        as `m_name`. **⇢** No `I` prefix on TypeScript interfaces, no `Trait`
        suffix on Rust traits.
      - Include units when they prevent ambiguity: `timeoutMs`, `distanceKm`.
        **⇢** Prefer a unit-carrying type where the language has one
        (`std::time::Duration`, `time.Duration`, an Effect `Duration`) and the
        suffix where it does not.
      - Named constants replace unexplained literals.
      - A name is unambiguous within its context.
  - id: "paradigms.restrictions-not-freedoms"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "Each programming paradigm takes something away, and architecture is built from the restrictions: disciplined control flow, disciplined indirection, disciplined mutation."
    body: |
      - Structured programming removes unrestrained `goto`.
      - Object-oriented programming removes unrestrained function pointers, which
        is what makes dependency inversion safe. **⇢** The fleet gets the same
        discipline from traits, interfaces and first-class functions typed by the
        compiler, with no inheritance (ADR-0010).
      - Functional programming removes unrestrained assignment. Immutability is
        the default; mutable state is segregated into components as small as
        possible; event sourcing is the extreme form of that segregation.
  - id: "prose.answer-first"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0026"
    source: "ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English (writing rules, not the dictionary); the caveman project's rules and benchmarks (github.com/JuliusBrussee/caveman, 2026-10); a measured 31,450-byte session hook"
    statement: "Write the result first, then the reason, then the next step. Do not open with what you will do. Do not close with a summary of what you said. Remove greetings, thanks and hedges that add no fact."
  - id: "prose.cut-by-deleting-not-by-grammar"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0026"
    source: "ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English (writing rules, not the dictionary); the caveman project's rules and benchmarks (github.com/JuliusBrussee/caveman, 2026-10); a measured 31,450-byte session hook"
    statement: "Make text shorter by deleting what carries no meaning: restated context, repeated facts, examples that teach nothing new. Do not drop articles, verbs or connectives to save words. Telegraphic instructions give worse answers (ADR-0026, Context). If a fragment is not shorter than the sentence, write the sentence."
  - id: "prose.full-sentences-where-misreading-costs"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0026"
    source: "ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English (writing rules, not the dictionary); the caveman project's rules and benchmarks (github.com/JuliusBrussee/caveman, 2026-10); a measured 31,450-byte session hook"
    statement: "Security warnings, irreversible actions and ordered steps are written in full sentences."
    body: |
      This also applies when the reader is confused. Every step stays in its
      order. Put the warning before the step it is about, not after.
  - id: "prose.meaning-floor"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0026"
    source: "ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English (writing rules, not the dictionary); the caveman project's rules and benchmarks (github.com/JuliusBrussee/caveman, 2026-10); a measured 31,450-byte session hook"
    statement: "Shortening never drops or changes negations, numbers, paths, code or error strings."
    body: |
      The full list: negations (*not*, *never*, *no*, *only*), numbers and
      units, paths, code, commands and error strings. Copy them character for
      character. If a shorter version loses one of these, keep the longer one.
  - id: "prose.one-idea-per-sentence"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0026"
    source: "ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English (writing rules, not the dictionary); the caveman project's rules and benchmarks (github.com/JuliusBrussee/caveman, 2026-10); a measured 31,450-byte session hook"
    statement: "Follow the STE sentence limits. A procedural sentence has 20 words or fewer and gives one instruction. A descriptive sentence has 25 words or fewer. A descriptive paragraph has six sentences or fewer. Use the active voice in procedures, and wherever possible elsewhere. Use one term for one thing, and do not change it for variety. Use a pronoun only when its referent is clear."
    body: |
      Code, paths, commands and tables are not sentences, and these limits do
      not apply to them.
  - id: "purity.calculations-from-actions"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "Calculations — pure functions from inputs to outputs — are kept separate from actions, and a calculation never performs or contains an action."
    body: |
      **Effect:** a domain function returns a plain value or an `Either`, and is
      called from inside an `Effect` rather than being one. **Rust:** a function
      that takes and returns values, no `&mut` to the outside world, no I/O; the
      I/O lives in the caller. **Go:** the same; a function with no `error` from
      I/O and no receiver holding a connection. The test is the one Grokking
      Simplicity gives: does it matter when or how often this runs? If not, it is
      a calculation and it must stay one.

      **Overrides:** ADR-0011's `data.object-or-structure` where it says an
      object exposes behaviour and hides data — here behaviour is a calculation
      over data, and the data is visible.
  - id: "purity.referential-transparency"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "A function is referentially transparent when possible: a call can be replaced by its result without changing the program."
    body: |
      Local mutation inside a function that is invisible from outside — a
      `let mut` accumulator in Rust, a loop variable in Go, a `for` over a local
      array in TypeScript — does not break this and is not a violation. What
      breaks it is observable state: a static, a captured mutable cell, a
      cache without a contract. *Functional Programming in Scala*'s substitution
      model is the test; *Functional Design* agrees, and says where the line was
      drawn too tightly in the past.
  - id: "resources.lifecycle-is-scoped"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "A resource that must be released — a connection, a file, a lock, a child process — is acquired and released by one construct that guarantees the release on every path, including failure and interruption."
    body: |
      **Effect:** `Scope`, `Effect.acquireRelease`, `Layer.scoped`. **Rust:**
      RAII and `Drop`; a guard type. **Go:** `defer` immediately after acquire,
      and `context` cancellation for anything long-lived. Manual pairing of
      open and close across functions is the smell.
  - id: "solid.dependency-inversion"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "Source-code dependencies point at abstractions the policy owns, never at volatile concretions: the concrete implementation depends on the interface, not the reverse."
    body: |
      Corollaries: do not refer to volatile concrete types; do not derive from
      them; do not override their functions; never mention the name of anything
      concrete and volatile. Concrete construction lives in a factory or the main
      component. **⇢** The consumer-side trait or interface in Rust and Go; the
      service tag plus a layer supplied from `main` in Effect.
  - id: "solid.interface-segregation"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "Depend on nothing you do not use: a fat interface couples its clients to changes they do not care about."
    body: |
      **⇢** Small traits and interfaces, declared by the consumer, composed when a
      caller needs several. Go's one-method interfaces are the model.
  - id: "solid.liskov-substitution"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "Anything that implements an interface can be substituted for anything else that does, without the client knowing which it has."
    body: |
      A violation forces the client to special-case implementations (the
      rectangle/square, the REST endpoint whose URL shape differs from its
      siblings). **⇢** Applies to trait and interface implementations exactly; a
      `match` on the concrete type behind a trait object, or a type assertion on
      an interface value, is the smell.
  - id: "solid.open-closed"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "A software artefact is open for extension and closed for modification: new behaviour arrives by adding code, not by changing working code."
    body: |
      Achieved by arranging dependencies so that higher-level components are
      protected from changes in lower-level ones. **⇢** A new trait or interface
      implementation, a new variant handled in one exhaustive match, a new Effect
      layer swapped in; never an edit to every caller.
  - id: "solid.single-responsibility"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Architecture (Robert C. Martin, 2017)"
    statement: "A module has one, and only one, reason to change: it is responsible to one actor."
    body: |
      Symptoms of violation: accidental duplication (two actors sharing code that
      then diverges) and merge conflicts between actors in one file. **⇢** A Rust
      module or Go package serves one actor; when two teams or two use cases keep
      editing the same file for different reasons, split it.
  - id: "style.expressions-over-statements"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "An expression that returns a value is preferred to a statement that mutates state: `const x = cond ? a : b` over `let x; if ... x = a`."
    body: |
      **Rust** is expression-oriented and makes this natural (`let x = match
      ...`); **TypeScript** has ternaries, `Match` and array methods; **Go** has
      neither, so the rule there is "compute a value in a function and return
      it" rather than fighting the language.
  - id: "style.no-cargo-cult-abstractions"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "No wrapper, type class, free monad or point-free contortion added only to look functional; an abstraction earns its place by removing a real duplication or a real risk."
    body: |
      Grokking Simplicity's warning, and *Functional Design*'s: the goal is code
      a colleague reads, not code that demonstrates the paradigm. An `Effect`
      around a pure function that never fails and needs nothing is this smell.
  - id: "style.pipelines-over-nesting"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "Explicit pipelines and early returns are preferred to deeply nested control flow."
    body: |
      Three levels of `if` is a prompt to extract a predicate, invert a condition
      and return early, or turn the branches into a match. In Effect, a nested
      `flatMap` ladder is rewritten as `Effect.gen`.
  - id: "systems.construction-in-main"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Object construction is separated from use: a composition root creates dependencies and passes them inward, and no business logic locates a service for itself."
    body: |
      **⇢** Effect `Layer`s assembled in `main`; Go constructors called once in
      `main`; Rust `main` building the concrete types and handing them to generic
      or trait-object code. Dependency injection separates construction from
      behaviour — constructor injection, services and layers, generics or trait
      objects — and there is no global service locator. Factories are used when
      the application must control creation without knowing concrete details.
  - id: "systems.grow-from-simple"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Start with a simple architecture that can grow, delay irreversible decisions until sufficient information exists, and preserve the ability to change technology choices."
    body: |
      - Cross-cutting concerns (transactions, persistence, security, telemetry)
        are separated from business rules.
      - Architectural decisions are test-driven where practical.
      - Decisions are made at the lowest responsible level rather than
        centralised.
      - Standards are adopted only where they provide demonstrable value; no
        framework or standard dominates the domain model.
      - A domain-specific language or expressive API is created where it
        clarifies important business concepts.
  - id: "testing.effectful-at-the-boundary"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "Effectful tests are kept around the interpreters and boundaries — a service implementation against a real or in-memory dependency — and stay few, because the logic they wrap has already been tested as calculations."
    body: |
      **Effect:** a test `Layer` providing an in-memory service, `TestClock`,
      `TestRandom`. **Rust:** an in-memory implementation of the trait. **Go:** a
      fake implementing the consumer's interface. ADR-0011's
      `tests.first-properties` still governs speed and independence.
  - id: "testing.property-based-for-invariants"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "Invariants are tested with property-based tests — generated inputs, a property that must hold for all of them — rather than with a handful of examples."
    body: |
      **Effect / TypeScript:** `fast-check` with `Schema.Arbitrary`. **Rust:**
      `proptest` or `quickcheck`. **Go:** `testing/quick` or `rapid`. Round-trip
      (`decode(encode(x)) == x`), idempotence and ordering properties are the
      usual first three.
  - id: "testing.pure-logic-directly"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "Pure logic is tested directly, as a function of inputs to outputs, with no mocks and no runtime."
    body: |
      A calculation that needs a test double to be tested has an action hiding
      inside it (`purity.calculations-from-actions`).
  - id: "tests.clean-as-production"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Test code is kept as clean as production code, refactored with the same care, readable through test-specific helpers or a small domain language."
    body: |
      - Each test communicates one concept; one logical assertion per test,
        though several physical assertions may express that one outcome.
      - Tests exercise observable behaviour, not implementation details.
      - Tests make refactoring and architectural change safe.
  - id: "tests.first-properties"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Tests are Fast, Independent, Repeatable, Self-validating and Timely: no dependence between tests or on execution order, no manual inspection as the pass/fail mechanism, and a suite fast enough to run at every commit."
    body: |
      **⇢** ADR-0009 runs the suite at pre-commit, which is the fleet's definition
      of "frequently". An ignored test is an unresolved question: `#[ignore]`,
      `t.Skip()`, `it.skip` each carry a reason and an owner, or they are deleted.
  - id: "tests.sufficient"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "The suite is sufficient to validate all expected behaviour: boundaries and edge cases are tested carefully, trivial tests are not skipped, and coverage tools find untested code without being taken as proof of quality."
    body: |
      - When a bug is found, nearby cases are tested; defects cluster.
      - Patterns across several failing tests are investigated; coverage patterns
        are read for clues about poor design.
  - id: "tests.tdd-rules"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "The three TDD rules: no production code until a failing test requires it; only enough test to demonstrate a failure; only enough production code to make that test pass."
  - id: "toolchain.a-directive-is-not-a-pin"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0019"
    source: "A staticcheck finding CI caught and a Go 1.27 workstation with a Go 1.25 linter did not, plus the 2026-09-21 survey of every first-party Go module; toolchain.language-servers-pinned from fleet-lsp's 2026-10-07 plan (language servers pinned per repository)"
    statement: "Language mechanisms that only set a floor or a hint — Go's `go` line and `toolchain` directive under the default `auto` policy, Node engines ranges, Python `requires-python` — are kept consistent with the pin but are not relied on to enforce it; two mechanisms that can disagree are one more thing to keep in step."
  - id: "toolchain.language-servers-pinned"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0019"
    source: "A staticcheck finding CI caught and a Go 1.27 workstation with a Go 1.25 linter did not, plus the 2026-09-21 survey of every first-party Go module; toolchain.language-servers-pinned from fleet-lsp's 2026-10-07 plan (language servers pinned per repository)"
    statement: "The language server an agent queries is pinned in the repository like its build tools, never taken from the machine's PATH: TypeScript's adapter as an exact `typescript-language-server` devDependency at the workspace root; Go's gopls as `tool golang.org/x/tools/gopls` in a separate `tools/go.mod` with its own `go.sum` (the default) or in the module's `go.mod`; Python's pyright as an exact `pyright==` requirement in `pyproject.toml`, matched by `uv.lock` when present. A language server is not a check-target tool, so `toolchain.tools-pinned-with-it` does not reach it, and no check target or CI runs it, so `toolchain.one-pin-read-by-both` does not apply: the pin is read by the agent's language-server broker (fleet-lsp) alone. One stated exception: gopls is built by the machine's Go (which must be at least the module's `go` line), because the module's own toolchain may be too old to build it. A new server version is measured for readiness before the repositories move to it, then they move in one batch."
  - id: "toolchain.one-pin-read-by-both"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0019"
    source: "A staticcheck finding CI caught and a Go 1.27 workstation with a Go 1.25 linter did not, plus the 2026-09-21 survey of every first-party Go module; toolchain.language-servers-pinned from fleet-lsp's 2026-10-07 plan (language servers pinned per repository)"
    statement: "A version is pinned once. Continuous integration runs the repository's own target (`make lint`, `make test`) rather than reinstalling the tool from a version of its own, so a workstation and the runner cannot drift apart."
  - id: "toolchain.pinned-exactly-in-the-repository"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0019"
    source: "A staticcheck finding CI caught and a Go 1.27 workstation with a Go 1.25 linter did not, plus the 2026-09-21 survey of every first-party Go module; toolchain.language-servers-pinned from fleet-lsp's 2026-10-07 plan (language servers pinned per repository)"
    statement: "A repository pins its language toolchain to an exact version — a patch version, not a floor — in a file the repository owns, and its check targets select that version themselves so the machine's PATH does not decide what compiles the code. A minimum version (`go 1.25`, `>=`) is not a pin."
  - id: "toolchain.tools-pinned-with-it"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0019"
    source: "A staticcheck finding CI caught and a Go 1.27 workstation with a Go 1.25 linter did not, plus the 2026-09-21 survey of every first-party Go module; toolchain.language-servers-pinned from fleet-lsp's 2026-10-07 plan (language servers pinned per repository)"
    statement: "Every tool a check target runs — linter, code generator, test harness — is pinned to an exact version in the same place as the toolchain and installed into the repository's ignored tool directory, never fetched `@latest` and never taken from the machine. A tool built against one language minor is bumped with that minor, and may be newer than the repository's toolchain but never older."
  - id: "types.document-the-contract"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "A function's type states its inputs, its outputs, the errors it can produce and the capabilities it requires, so the signature is the documentation."
    body: |
      **Effect:** `Effect<A, E, R>` says all four. **Rust:** `fn f(input: In) ->
      Result<Out, Error>` with the error enum listing every case, and trait
      bounds naming what it needs. **Go:** `func F(ctx, in In) (Out, error)` with
      a documented sentinel or error type set; the weaker type system means the
      doc comment carries what the type cannot. A signature that takes `any` or
      returns `unknown` has declined to say.
  - id: "types.exhaustive-matching"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "Every match over a union is exhaustive, and adding a variant is a compile error or a lint failure at every match that does not handle it."
    body: |
      **Effect / TypeScript:** `Match.exhaustive`, or a `switch` whose `default`
      assigns to `never`; `noFallthroughCasesInSwitch` on. **Rust:** the compiler
      enforces it; a `_ =>` arm on a domain enum is a review comment because it
      defeats the check. **Go:** the compiler does not enforce it; the
      `exhaustive` linter is on in CI and a type switch has no silent `default`.
  - id: "types.illegal-states-unrepresentable"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "The types are shaped so that an invalid state is difficult or impossible to construct, so that validation happens once, at construction, and every function downstream can trust its input."
    body: |
      **Effect / TypeScript:** a discriminated union with one variant per valid
      state, and a `Schema` that only parses into valid ones. **Rust:** an
      `enum` per state, fields present only in the variants that have them, a
      constructor returning `Result`. **Go:** the language allows less; use
      unexported fields plus a validating constructor, and a small interface with
      one implementation per state where a union is wanted. This is a heuristic
      because the last ten percent costs more than it returns; the constraint
      form of the idea is `types.variants-as-unions` below.
  - id: "types.variants-as-unions"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "Variants are modelled as a discriminated union — one case per alternative, each carrying only its own data — never as boolean flags or optional fields whose combinations must be checked."
    body: |
      **Effect / TypeScript:** a tagged union (`_tag`), `Data.TaggedEnum`,
      `Schema.Union`. **Rust:** an `enum` with data-carrying variants. **Go:**
      a sealed interface with one struct per case, or a kind field plus a
      type switch kept in one place. A struct with `isPaid: boolean;
      paidAt?: Date; refundedAt?: Date` is the smell; `Unpaid | Paid { paidAt }
      | Refunded { paidAt, refundedAt }` is the rule.

      **Overrides:** nothing in ADR-0011; it is the data-modelling form of
      `functions.no-flag-arguments`.
  - id: "units.low-coupling"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "Units depend on abstractions rather than on volatile concrete details, are isolated from external systems likely to change, and admit new behaviour by addition rather than by modifying stable code."
    body: |
      **⇢** Open–Closed here means a new trait implementation, a new interface
      satisfier, a new variant handled in one match — not an edit to every
      caller. No feature envy: behaviour lives near the data it primarily
      manipulates; responsibilities sit where readers expect to find them. No
      free function that hard-codes one implementation where a trait or
      interface parameter would let a caller substitute one (the book's
      "inappropriate static method").
  - id: "units.small-and-cohesive"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0011"
    source: "Clean Code (Robert C. Martin, 2008)"
    statement: "A unit is small — measured in responsibilities, not lines — has one reason to change, and is cohesive: its fields and functions belong together."
    body: |
      **⇢** Read "class" throughout as the unit the language offers: a Rust module
      or struct with its `impl`, a Go package or type with methods, a TypeScript
      module or an Effect service. ADR-0010 rules out inheritance hierarchies;
      size, cohesion and coupling survive intact.

      - A description containing "and", "if", "or" or "but" signals multiple
        responsibilities.
      - A unit is split when subsets of its functions operate on unrelated
        subsets of its fields.
      - Internal details are encapsulated, with carefully scoped test access
        where necessary. **⇢** `pub(crate)`, an internal package, an `@internal`
        export.
      - Members are organised predictably; public APIs are small.
      - Dead units, functions and fields are removed.
  - id: "values.immutable-by-default"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "Values are immutable by default; a value that must change is copied with the change applied, and mutation is confined to a place that owns the value and is visible from its signature."
    body: |
      **Effect / TypeScript:** `readonly` fields and arrays, `Data.struct`,
      `Schema.Class` values; update by spread or `Struct.evolve`, never in place.
      **Rust:** bindings are immutable unless `mut`, and a `&mut` parameter is the
      signature saying so — the rule is already the language's; keep `mut`
      scoped to the function that needs it. **Go:** no mutation of a slice or
      map received as an argument; return a new one, or take ownership by name
      (`appendTo`). Grokking Simplicity's copy-on-write and defensive copying are
      the mechanisms where the language does not enforce it.
  - id: "values.no-primitive-obsession"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "A domain concept is a domain type, not a bare string, number or boolean: an `Isin`, not a `string`; a `Quantity`, not a `number`."
    body: |
      **Effect:** `Schema.brand` and branded types; `Schema.Class` for
      structured values. **Rust:** a newtype `struct Isin(String)` with a
      validating constructor. **Go:** a named type `type Isin string` with a
      constructor that validates and is the only way to make one. *Domain
      Modeling Made Functional*'s first move: the type system documents the
      domain, and a function that takes an `Isin` cannot be handed an order id.
  - id: "work.agent-lands-the-plan"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0029"
    source: "22 release questions approved 22 times with the hold option never chosen, and an agent that handed a deploy back to the person (2026-09-08 to 2026-10-08)"
    statement: "After the person approves a plan, the agent carries it to production without handing it back. Each of its pull requests merges under `work.merge-on-green`, and the merge is what deploys: GitOps applies it. The agent then continues, in the same task, with what the plan says comes next — the release a later phase needs, the deploy, the live phase — and does not stop to ask the person to confirm a step the plan names."
  - id: "work.agent-observes-the-live-phase"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0029"
    source: "22 release questions approved 22 times with the hold option never chosen, and an agent that handed a deploy back to the person (2026-09-08 to 2026-10-08)"
    statement: 'A phase that checks the live system — a forced first run, a smoke test, a soak, a revocation that must be confirmed — is run and observed by the agent, which records input, expected and observed in the plan. A plan does not add "a person watches" to such a phase unless the person asked for it.'
  - id: "work.approval-covers-one-commit"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0028"
    source: "Interface regressions found by the person after deploy despite agent browser checks (2026-09)"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: "An approval covers the commit that was previewed and nothing after it. A later commit, amend or rebase goes back through the commit gate, the verification loop and a new preview. Dependencies for the preview are installed from the lockfile, frozen, so the worktree stays clean. When the plan declares evidence, the guide and evidence in the pull request cover the commit they were made from in the same way."
  - id: "work.ask-only-what-the-plan-left-open"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0029"
    source: "22 release questions approved 22 times with the hold option never chosen, and an agent that handed a deploy back to the person (2026-09-08 to 2026-10-08)"
    statement: "While carrying an approved plan, the agent asks the person only for what the plan did not decide: a product or design choice, work outside its phases, a destructive or irreversible step the plan does not name (deleting data, force-pushing, revoking or rotating a credential outside the plan), a `rework` that survives its Delta, or an interface preview ADR-0028 still requires. A step the plan names is not asked again."
  - id: "work.ceremony-scales-with-size"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0022"
    source: "Plan-mode plans left outside every repository, four incompatible in-repo plan layouts, and interface regressions found after deploy (2026-09); Oxide RFD 1 and OpenAI's ExecPlans"
    statement: "A change describable in one sentence gets no plan and joins the open batch branch. A bug gets current, expected and unchanged behaviour in its commit body. Anything larger gets a plan. A deliberate shortcut carries its `holds-until:` comment in the code (`change.a-deferral-names-its-ceiling`), and the plan's Decision log points to it when a plan exists."
  - id: "work.commit-body-says-why"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0022"
    source: "Plan-mode plans left outside every repository, four incompatible in-repo plan layouts, and interface regressions found after deploy (2026-09); Oxide RFD 1 and OpenAI's ExecPlans"
    statement: "The subject says what changed. The body says why, and only when the subject and the diff do not make it clear. The body does not list what the diff already shows: files, rule ids, renamed symbols. A bug fix keeps its current, expected and unchanged behaviour (`work.ceremony-scales-with-size`)."
  - id: "work.commits-follow-phases"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0022"
    source: "Plan-mode plans left outside every repository, four incompatible in-repo plan layouts, and interface regressions found after deploy (2026-09); Oxide RFD 1 and OpenAI's ExecPlans"
    statement: "A plan's phases become commits, with structural and behavioural changes in separate commits, each passing the commit gate."
  - id: "work.findings-one-per-line"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0022"
    source: "Plan-mode plans left outside every repository, four incompatible in-repo plan layouts, and interface regressions found after deploy (2026-09); Oxide RFD 1 and OpenAI's ExecPlans"
    statement: "A reviewer that reports in free text starts with one line, `Verdict: <verdict>`. Then each finding is one line, most severe first: `path:line severity — defect; rule broken. Fix.` The severity is one of `blocking`, `high`, `medium` or `low`. A finding with no line names the file or the plan section instead. Evidence follows on at most two indented lines, and only when the location does not show the defect. What the reviewer could not check goes in a separate last list, not among the findings."
    body: |
      The review agents that `work.plan-review-panel` and
      `work.implementation-review` launch report in this format. A tool that
      reports through a structured interface, such as a findings tool, keeps
      that interface.
  - id: "work.human-decisions-offer-options"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0022"
    source: "Plan-mode plans left outside every repository, four incompatible in-repo plan layouts, and interface regressions found after deploy (2026-09); Oxide RFD 1 and OpenAI's ExecPlans"
    statement: "When the work needs a person's decision, prefer two to four meaningful options, with a recommendation where one is justified, and always accept a free-text correction. Ask an open question when the missing information cannot be represented honestly as options. A plan marks its decision points as `🧑 decision:` in its Phases."
  - id: "work.implementation-review"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0022"
    source: "Plan-mode plans left outside every repository, four incompatible in-repo plan layouts, and interface regressions found after deploy (2026-09); Oxide RFD 1 and OpenAI's ExecPlans"
    statement: "Before the push of an implementation branch that carries a plan, one independent reviewer has read the diff against that plan and the repository's active rules, and its findings are recorded in the plan. The reviewer is not the author: it reads and reports, and it never edits, waives or approves anything. It checks that each ticked phase is implemented as the plan's Behaviour says and nothing else is, that each recorded verification names an input, an expected and an actual observation the diff can produce, that no active constraint is violated (each finding names the rule and `file:line`), that no added test is one that cannot fail, and that nothing lies outside the phases or inside the Non-goals without a `holds-until:` comment."
    body: |
      The review binds to the tree it read, without the plan directory, so
      recording it in the plan never makes it stale, and a later code change
      does. Every review ends with a verdict (`approve`,
      `approve-with-changes` or `rework`) and findings that each carry evidence
      and the concrete edit.

      Rounds are bounded: one review, then one delta on the tree that fixes its
      findings. There is no third: a `rework` that survives the delta goes to
      the person as a decision with both positions, and only the person may
      overrule it. The plan carries the verdict, what was fixed and what was
      kept as deliberate, in at most five lines.

      A branch with no plan needs no implementation review
      (`work.ceremony-scales-with-size`). A relais run keeps its own reviewer
      against its contract.
  - id: "work.lasting-decisions-become-adrs"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0022"
    source: "Plan-mode plans left outside every repository, four incompatible in-repo plan layouts, and interface regressions found after deploy (2026-09); Oxide RFD 1 and OpenAI's ExecPlans"
    statement: "A Decision-log entry that will still bind future changes after the plan is done becomes an ADR in the same pull request, and the log links to it."
  - id: "work.merge-on-green"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0022"
    source: "Plan-mode plans left outside every repository, four incompatible in-repo plan layouts, and interface regressions found after deploy (2026-09); Oxide RFD 1 and OpenAI's ExecPlans"
    statement: "The agent that opened an implementation pull request merges it without asking, once every check on its head has completed with success. The merge is a separate step, taken after the result is read (`ci.gates-the-merge`), and only when these conditions hold:"
    body: |
      - the pull request's implementation review (`work.implementation-review`)
        is `approve`, or `approve-with-changes` with every finding fixed or named
        deliberate;
      - the pull request that it follows has merged. A pull request names the one
        it follows with `merge-after: <repo>#<n>` in its body; when that one also
        needs a release before it can work, the release must be out first;
      - the person has not said to hold it, in the session or in the pull request.

      A red check is a report under the repair budget of
      `work.verification-loop-before-push`, never a reason to retry until green.
      A merge alone never cuts a release (`work.release-when-the-plan-needs-it`), and a decision
      left open with the person does not hold back a merge that does not depend
      on it.
  - id: "work.one-implementation-pr-per-repo-per-plan"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0022"
    source: "Plan-mode plans left outside every repository, four incompatible in-repo plan layouts, and interface regressions found after deploy (2026-09); Oxide RFD 1 and OpenAI's ExecPlans"
    statement: "One plan produces one implementation pull request per repository it touches, carrying the plan's phases as commits. One explicit closing pull request is allowed for cross-repository completion or soak evidence. Any other exception is recorded in the plan's Decision log. Each of these pull requests merges under `work.merge-on-green`."
  - id: "work.plan-closes-with-the-branch"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0022"
    source: "Plan-mode plans left outside every repository, four incompatible in-repo plan layouts, and interface regressions found after deploy (2026-09); Oxide RFD 1 and OpenAI's ExecPlans"
    statement: "The last commit of a task branch closes its plan for that branch, as the first commit opens it. `status` becomes `done` when the pull request completes the last phase, or stays `active` with the next phase named. Every Verification entry carries the actual result observed before the push. No entry waits on CI, the merge or a release: those outcomes belong to the pull request's checks and the tag, never to a pending line in the plan. A pointer file closes its slice the same way."
  - id: "work.plan-lifecycle"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0022"
    source: "Plan-mode plans left outside every repository, four incompatible in-repo plan layouts, and interface regressions found after deploy (2026-09); Oxide RFD 1 and OpenAI's ExecPlans"
    statement: "`active` while phases are open: phases are ticked, and the Decision log, Verification and Outcome sections are appended to. `done` when every phase is implemented and verified. `abandoned` when the work stops, with the reason in Outcome. `imported` for a legacy plan whose completion cannot be established. The last three are frozen, and a follow-up is a new plan. Merge state is never written into the file."
  - id: "work.plan-lives-in-the-repo"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0022"
    source: "Plan-mode plans left outside every repository, four incompatible in-repo plan layouts, and interface regressions found after deploy (2026-09); Oxide RFD 1 and OpenAI's ExecPlans"
    statement: "An approved plan is committed as the first commit of its task branch, in `docs/plans/`, named by approval date and slug, from the explicit path the approving session was given, never the newest file in a directory. A plan spanning repositories lives in the one that owns its decision; the others carry a pointer file of the same name."
  - id: "work.plan-review-panel"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0022"
    source: "Plan-mode plans left outside every repository, four incompatible in-repo plan layouts, and interface regressions found after deploy (2026-09); Oxide RFD 1 and OpenAI's ExecPlans"
    statement: "Before a plan is presented for approval, a panel of expert reviewers has reviewed it and its findings are folded in. The panel follows what the plan touches:"
    body: |
      | When | Reviewers |
      |---|---|
      | always | an expert in each language of the project; a staff backend engineer |
      | Kubernetes / ops | a staff platform engineer |
      | a command line | a Unix expert; a TUI expert |
      | a large or long-lived repository | a product owner; an architect |
      | an interface | a React staff engineer; a UI designer; a UX researcher who backs the plan with cited research; a developer/UX designer from the video game industry |

      The areas are read from each repository's structure on its default branch,
      never from keywords in the plan, plus the areas the plan declares it adds.
      A repository is large at 500 commits or 500 files, counting only its own
      history and excluding vendored, lock and generated paths.

      Every review ends with a verdict (`approve`, `approve-with-changes` or
      `rework`), findings that each carry evidence and the concrete edit, and
      what the reviewer would measure. The panel reviews the plan's canonical
      body: the plan without its review section, its full reviews and its
      machine comment, so writing review metadata never makes a review stale.

      Rounds are bounded. The first round is the whole panel. The second runs
      only the reviewers who asked for rework, plus the backend reviewer, on the
      final body. There is no third round: what stays unresolved goes to the
      person as a decision with both positions. A change the person asks for gets
      one delta review, by the backend reviewer and the reviewers of every area
      the change touches or adds, and a rework from that delta becomes a decision,
      never another round.

      The plan opens with a review section of at most five lines: what the person
      must decide, the repositories and the panel, what the review changed, and
      the verdicts. The full reviews follow at the end, as a plain section. A
      review the person asked to skip is marked unreviewed there. The review
      still rejects a plan whose Verification section is not made of observable
      checks.
  - id: "work.plans-are-not-context"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0022"
    source: "Plan-mode plans left outside every repository, four incompatible in-repo plan layouts, and interface regressions found after deploy (2026-09); Oxide RFD 1 and OpenAI's ExecPlans"
    statement: "`docs/plans/` is history and is read on demand. It is never loaded into every session: an old plan competes with the instructions that are current."
  - id: "work.preview-is-guided"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0028"
    source: "Interface regressions found by the person after deploy despite agent browser checks (2026-09)"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: "A preview reaches the person as a guide, not as a question. They work on several projects at once and do not remember where this one stood, so the guide is self-contained. It gives:"
    body: |
      - where we are (project, branch and plan, and why they are asked);
      - what they should see, including "nothing new" when that is the point;
      - numbered steps: the exact URL, each click named by its visible label and
        position, and after each step what should appear;
      - before and after screenshots;
      - what the agent already checked, and what to look at especially.

      The same guide is written to a local page beside the screenshots. The app is
      opened for the person in their browser at the state the first step reaches.
      Only then is the verdict asked. An approval given without a guide is not an
      informed one: ask again with the guide before publishing. When the plan
      declares evidence (`work.preview-unless-planned-evidence`), the same guide,
      screenshots included, goes in the pull request. No local page is opened and
      no question is asked.
  - id: "work.preview-unless-planned-evidence"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0028"
    source: "Interface regressions found by the person after deploy despite agent browser checks (2026-09)"
    applies:
      - "ui"
    statement: "A commit that changes the interface is published only after the person approves it on localhost, unless the plan the person approved declares that the evidence leaves nothing to judge. The agent verifies it first (`work.verification-loop-before-push`). It then serves the clean worktree at that commit, shows the URL, its verification record and the changed behaviour, and asks for a verdict: Approve, Request changes or Hold. The person describes any requested changes; the agent does not guess them into an option."
    body: |
      The declaration is a `## Preview` section in the plan body whose first
      non-empty line starts with `evidence: <reason>`. It is never in front
      matter, which the approved hash does not cover. With it, the agent writes
      the guide as usual, puts it and the evidence in the pull request, and asks
      nothing. The exemption lapses, and the question is asked, when the
      screenshot comparison shows a visible change the plan did not decide. It
      never covers screens drawn from a picked mockup (ADR-0027). The approval
      question names its preview by id: the id begins with the commit's short
      sha.
  - id: "work.release-when-the-plan-needs-it"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0029"
    source: "22 release questions approved 22 times with the hold option never chosen, and an agent that handed a deploy back to the person (2026-09-08 to 2026-10-08)"
    statement: "A release is cut when the person asks for one, or when an approved plan needs it: a later phase deploys the released artifact, or the plan's outcome is the release. The agent cuts it through the `tag-release` skill and verifies the published artifact, without asking. A release is never cut automatically per pull request otherwise. ADR-0008 still decides what triggers the publish: a pushed version tag."
  - id: "work.verification-loop-before-push"
    level: "constraint"
    adopts: "ADR-0022"
    source: "Plan-mode plans left outside every repository, four incompatible in-repo plan layouts, and interface regressions found after deploy (2026-09); Oxide RFD 1 and OpenAI's ExecPlans"
    statement: "Before any push, the changed behaviour is verified through an appropriate observable check, and the input, the expected observation and the actual result are recorded in the plan and the pull request. For an interface, that means driving the changed route in a real browser. For a command, a service, an operator or manifests, it means running the real thing on a real input. A focused test of the public behaviour can itself be the check for a library, test-only or documentation change. A green general suite alone is not a check."
    body: |
      A failure gets one initial attempt plus at most three repair attempts on the
      same unresolved failure, and then a report instead of a push. Missing
      credentials or unavailable infrastructure produce a blocker report, not
      speculative fixes. Changes the person asks for do not count against the
      repair budget.
  - id: "workflow.pipeline-of-transformations"
    level: "heuristic"
    adopts: "ADR-0015"
    source: "Grokking Simplicity (Normand, 2021); Domain Modeling Made Functional (Wlaschin, 2018); Functional Programming in Scala (Chiusano and Bjarnason); Functional Design (Martin, 2023)"
    statement: "A workflow is a pipeline of small transformations composed into a whole, each step taking the previous step's output as a value."
    body: |
      **Effect:** `pipe`, `Effect.flatMap`, `Effect.gen` reading top to bottom.
      **Rust:** iterator chains, `?` chains, `and_then`. **Go:** a sequence of
      calls in one function with early returns, each producing a named value —
      Go has no pipe, so the pipeline is the reading order. *Domain Modeling Made
      Functional*'s workflow shape: validate, then transform, then act.
